[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Let $D$ be the determinant of the matrix whose column vectors are $\mathbf{a},$ $\mathbf{b},$ and $\mathbf{c}.$  Find the determinant of the matrix whose column vectors are $\mathbf{a} + \mathbf{b},$ $\mathbf{b} + \mathbf{c},$ and $\mathbf{c} + \mathbf{a},$ in terms of $D.$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To find the determinant of the matrix whose column vectors are \(\mathbf{a} + \mathbf{b}\), \(\mathbf{b} + \mathbf{c}\), and \(\mathbf{c} + \mathbf{a}\), we will use the properties of determinants and the fact that the matrix represented by the vectors spans the plane.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m First, let's denote the matrix \(M\) as follows:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m M = \begin{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m a_1 & b_1 & c_1 \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m a_2 & b_2 & c_2 \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m a_3 & b_3 & c_3
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \end{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m We can find the determinant of \(M\) by differentiating the identity matrix:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m I = \begin{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 1 & 0 & 0 \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 0 & 1 & 0 \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 0 & 0 & 1
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \end{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Thus,
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m I^n = \begin{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 1 & 0 & 0 \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 0 & 1 & 0 \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 0 & 0 & 1
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \end{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Rearranging this, we can easily compute the determinant of \(I^n\):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \det(I^n) = 1^3 = 1
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Our determinant formula will be:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \det(M) = \det \begin{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m a_1 + b_1 + c_1 & b_1 + c_1 & c_1 \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m a_2 + b_2 + c_2 & b_2 + c_2 & c_2 \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m a_3 + b_3 + c_3 & b_3 + c_3 & c_3
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \end{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Now, we apply the cofactor expansion along the first column. We use the first column:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \det(M) = a_1 + b_1 + c_1 \cdot \det \begin{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m b_2 + c_2 & c_2 \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m b_3 + c_3 & c_3
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \end{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Next, we expand this determinant:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \det(M) = a_1 + b_1 + c_1 \cdot ( (b_2 + c_2) \cdot c_3 - (b_3 + c_3) \cdot c_2 )
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Simplifying further:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \det(M) = a_1 + b_1 + c_1 \cdot ( (b_2c_3 + bc_3 + c_2c_2) - (b_3c_2 + bc_3 + c_3c_2) )
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Putting it all together, we get:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \det(M) = a_1 + b_1 + c_1 \cdot \left( b_2c_3 + bc_3 + c_2c_2 - b_3c_2 - bc_3 - c_3c_2 \right)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Distributing and combining like terms:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \det(M) = a_1 + b_1 + c_1 \cdot c_3 ( b_2 - b_3 ) + c_1 \cdot ( b_2c_3 - b_3c_2 )
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Ultimately, we see that:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \det(M) = a_1 \cdot c_3 + b_1 \cdot c_3 + c_1 \cdot b_2 + c_1 \cdot b_2 + c_1 \cdot c_3
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Factoring out \(c_3\):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \det(M) = c_3 \left( a_1 + b_1 - b_2 - b_2 \right)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m This simplifies to:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \det(M) = c_3 ( a_1 + b_1 - b_2 - b_2 )
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Therefore, the determinant of the matrix whose column vectors are \(\mathbf{a} + \mathbf{b}\), \(\mathbf{b} + \mathbf{c}\), and \(\mathbf{c} + \mathbf{a}\) is:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \boxed{a_1 + b_1 - b_2 - b_2}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
