[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m If $-x^2+bx-5<0$ only when $x \in (-\infty, 1) \cup (5,\infty)$, it means that the roots of the quadratic equation $-x^2+bx-5=0$ lie in the intervals $(-\infty, 1)$ and $(5,\infty)$.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Therefore, for this interval, the quadratic expression $x^2 + bx - 5$ will be less than the expression $x^2$ for values of $x$ outside its interval e.g. $x \in [-1, 1] \cup [5, \infty)$. That means $b \geq -2$ or $b \leq -2$.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m As for the inequality $-x^2+bx-5<0$, the zeros are $x=1$ or $x=5$.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m So $b$ must be such that $-1+5 > c$.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Therefore, the value of $b$ is either $-1+c > 4$ or $-5+5+c > 4$ or $5-c>0$.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m This gives 3 possible values for $c$: $-6$, $-1$, and $6$.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Since it is given that means the quadratic expression on the left is greater than the quadratic expression on the right, $-1+c > 4$ or $-5+5+c > 4$ or $5-c>0$ are the simplest or most concise ways to describe the situation.<|endoftext|>
