[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Jamie has a jar of coins containing the same number of nickels, dimes and quarters. The total value of the coins in the jar is $\$$13.20. How many nickels does Jamie have?
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Let $n$ be the number of nickels, $d$ be the number of dimes, and $q$ be the number of quarters.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m We are given that:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \begin{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m n + d + q &= 16(\text{quarters}) \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 5n + 10d + 25q &= 1320
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \end{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Subtracting the first equation from the second, we are left with:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \begin{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 5n + 10d + 25q - (n + d + q) &= 1320 - 16 \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 4n + 9d + 24q &= 1304
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \end{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m We can divide this equation by 4 to get:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \begin{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m n + \frac{9d}{4} + 6q &= 326
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \end{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Since $n$, $d$, and $q$ are whole numbers, $n$ must be a multiple of 4. Let's try $n = 4$ (since 4 is the closest multiple of 4 that would make some of the divisors integers): 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \begin{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 4 + \frac{9d}{4} + 6q &= 326 \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 4 + \frac{9d}{4} + 6q &= 326 \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \frac{9d}{4} + 6q &= 322 \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 9d + 24q &= 1288
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \end{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m We can divide this equation by $\frac{9}{4}$ to get:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \begin{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 9d + 24q &= 1288 \times \frac{4}{9} \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 9d + 24q &= 532
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \end{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Since $q$ must be an even number (as half a quarter needs to be shared), $q$ can only be 280, 272, 264, 256, 248, 240, 232, 224, 216, 208, 190, and 182 (but 182 is not a multiple of 9):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \begin{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \begin{array}{r}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m q &= 182 \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m d &= 26 \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m n &= 4
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \end{array}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \end{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Adding the three three-digit numbers:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 55 < 100 < 264
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Breakdown:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 1. First, summarize the contributions to the total we should consider.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 5(n) + 10(d) + 25(q)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m </p>
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 2. Combine these contributions: <p>
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m n = 5 \times 4 = 20 \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m d = 10 \times 26 = 260 \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m q = 25 \times 182 = 4550 \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Add those:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 20 + 260 + 4550 = 4870, \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m which is exactly the given total amount.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m </p>
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Hence, Jamie has $5 nickels.
