[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m First, we need to identify the parameters in the given trigonometric function $y = a \sin (bx + c) + d.$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m In the expression $a \sin (bx + c) + d,$ we can identify:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m * $a$: The amplitude of the motion is the maximum displacement from the equilibrium position. In this case, the amplitude is 2, as the graph shows two complete periods. So, $a=2$.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m * $b$: The period of the function is given as $\pi/3.$ The period of $\sin (bx + c)$ is $2p/b,$ where $p$ is the period of the sine function ($b$). Thus, we have:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    $$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    \frac{\pi}{b} = \frac{\pi}{3} \implies b = 3
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    $$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m * $c$: The phase shift of the function is $-pi.$ The general form of a sine function is $\sin (x + c),$ where $c$ acts as the phase shift. So, its value is $c = -pi.$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Next, we need to determine the amplitude, maximum value, and minimum value of the function. The amplitude, which is the maximum value minus the minimum value (centroid), is given by:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \text{Amplitude} = |\text{Max} - \text{Min}|
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m We can find the maximum and minimum values of the sine function by graphically inspecting the graph. The maximum value is $a = 2,$ since the highest point in the function occurs at the highest point on the graph.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m We know the function $\sin (3x - pi)$ has a period of $\frac{2\pi}{3}$. We also know the amplitude of the captured graph is 2, so we can find the centroid by adding the amplitude to the function's maximum:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m (\text{Max}) + \text{Amplitude} = \sqrt{(-3+3)^2 + (2)^2 + (-3-2)^2}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Computing this gives:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m (\text{Max}) + 2 = \pm \sqrt{25} = \pm 5
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Since $(-3+3)^2 + (2)^2 + (-3-2)^2$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \ge -12
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Therefore,
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \text{Centroid} = 5
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m For the minimum value, we can get the minimum value by graphing the function on a graphing utility:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m (-2+2)^2 + (2)^2 - (-3-2)^2
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Computing this gives $-2$.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m So, given the parameters $a=2,$ $b=3,$ $c=-\pi,$ and $\text{Amplitude} = 2,$ the graph of $y=a \sin (bx + c)+d$ should be:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m <img src="https://i.imgur.com/ffy3pp5.png" style="width:70%;"/>From this graph, we can find the $y$-coordinate at the centroid, which is $5$.<|endoftext|>
