[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m We have a total of 24 cats. Let's call this number \(C\).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Ten of the cats have black fur, and their number is \(B\), so \(B = 10\).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m We want to find the smallest possible number of cats that both have black fur and catch mice.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Since the number of cats that have black fur can be any integer from 1 to B (because cat = $24$ cats, it cannot be more than 24). Also since we want this number to also be a solution to the problem, it will be a subset of \(B\) (because we can't have more cats with black fur than there are actually black cats).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m So, let's consider the smallest possible number of cats that have black fur (since we want to find the intersection of these two sets and the size of that intersection is what we want), which is \(B = 10\).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Now, we know that there are 24 cats in total. If 10 of them have black fur and 10 cats have no mice, then we have \(10 + 10 = 20\) cats that have both a black fur cat and do not catch mice.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Therefore, the smallest possible number of cats that do not catch mice that have black fur is \(\boxed{20}\).
