[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m What is the area, in square units, of an isosceles trapezoid with bases of 7 and 17 units and legs of 13 units?
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To find the area of the isosceles trapezoid, we can use the formula for the area of a trapezoid, which is:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Area = (base1 + base2) / 2 * height
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m In this case, the bases are 7 and 17 units, so the sum of the bases is:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m sum_of_bases = base1 + base2 = 7 + 17 = 24 units.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m The legs are each 13 units long, so the length of the height (h) can be found using the Pythagorean theorem:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m { h }^{ 2 } = { (7 / 2)}^{ 2 } + { (17 / 2)}^{ 2 }
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m { h }^{ 2 } = { 241/4 }- 169/4
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m { h }^{ 2 } = { 241-169\over 4 }
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m { h }^{ 2 } = { 72\over 4 }
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m { h }^{ 2 } = { 18\over 1 }
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m { h }^{ 2 } = { 2\sqrt{9\cdot 2} }
