[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To find the remainder when \(2^{19})^{}\) is divided by 7 using modular arithmetic, we start by recognizing that any power of 2 will cycle through remainders based on the given number system. This is because in an odd-powered base (like 2), the remainder when an integer is divided by the base repeatedly will decrease cyclically.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Let's denote the remainder when \(2^k\) is divided by 7 as \(r_k\). We need to find the pattern of \(r_k\):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 1. \(2^1 = 2 \Rightarrow r_1 = 2\)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 2. \(2^2 = 4 \Rightarrow r_2 = 4\)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 3. \(2^3 = 8 \Rightarrow r_3 = 1\)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 4. \(2^4 = 16 \Rightarrow r_4 = 6\)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 5. \(2^5 = 32 \Rightarrow r_5 = 2\)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 6. \(2^6 = 64 \Rightarrow r_6 = 4\)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m We observe that \(r_2 = 4\) and then \(2 = 4 \times 2\), so \(r_7 = 1\) (since \(r_7 = 2^3 = 8 \mod 7\)). Therefore, we find that the remainder repeats every 3 powers. Hence, \(2^{19} = (2^3)^k \Rightarrow r_k = 1\) for all \(1 \le k \le 3\).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Thus, the remainder when \(2^{19}\) is divided by 7 is:\]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \(\boxed{1}\)<|endoftext|>
