[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To simplify the expression, we can use trigonometric identities and properties of roots of unity. Let's start by rewriting the terms in a more convenient form for the trigonometric function.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \cos \frac{2\pi}{13} + \cos \frac{6\pi}{13} + \cos \frac{8\pi}{13}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Using the sum-to-product identities, we know that the sum of cosines of the form \(\cos \left( \frac{(n+1)\pi}{2n} \right)\) and \(\cos \left( \frac{(n-1)\pi}{2n} \right)\) can be simplified when \(n\) is an even number. Here, \(n = 6\) (since \(n = 8-2\)), so we need to express \(\cos \frac{20\pi}{26}\) and \(\cos \frac{14\pi}{26}\) in terms of even-numbered cosine terms.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m First, rationalize one of the angles:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \cos \frac{14\pi}{26} = \cos \frac{7\pi}{13}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \cos \frac{20\pi}{26} = \cos \frac{6\pi}{13} = \cos \frac{2\pi}{13} - i \sin \frac{2\pi}{13}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Now, we can use the cosine addition formula for two angles with arguments \(\pi/13\) and \(-\pi/13\), and sum them up for three times:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \cos \left( \frac{20\pi}{26} \right) + \cos \left( \frac{6\pi}{13} \right) = \cos \left( \frac{20\pi}{26} + \frac{6\pi}{13} \right) = \cos \left( \frac{20\pi}{26} + \frac{12\pi}{26} \right) = \cos \left( \frac{32\pi}{26} \right) = \cos \left( \frac{16\pi}{13} \right)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \cos \left( \frac{20\pi}{26} - \frac{6\pi}{13} \right) = \cos \left( \frac{20\pi}{26} - \frac{12\pi}{26} \right) = \cos \left( \frac{8\pi}{26} \right) = \cos \left( \frac{4\pi}{13} \right)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m So, the expression becomes:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 2 \left( \cos \frac{16\pi}{13} + i \sin \frac{16\pi}{13} \right)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Next, consider the exponential form \(e^{i\theta}\). Note that:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \cos \frac{16\pi}{13} + i \sin \frac{16\pi}{13} = e^{i\left( \frac{16\pi}{13} - \frac{\pi}{2} \right)} = e^{-\frac{5\pi}{13}}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Thus, the expression simplifies to:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \left( e^{-\frac{5\pi}{13}} \right)^2 = e^{-\frac{10\pi}{13}}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m The final answer is:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \boxed{\frac{1}{e^{\frac{10\pi}{13}}}}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
