[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m The error arises because we are trying to evaluate a symbolic expression as an equality in Python, which is not legal. Instead, let's simplify the problem by directly visualizing the division of the trapezium into four equal area quadrilaterals. We know that the diagonals of the trapezoid bisect each other and divide the trapezium into two parallelograms (or triangles), each with half the area of the trapezium.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m In this specific problem, the trapezium (AD) can be divided into two quadrilaterals (DEP and DBQ) each divided into two equal triangles. Each of these triangles has an area equal to half the area of the trapezium. 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To confirm this, let's consider the following:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 1. Let's label the vertices of the trapezium as A, B, C, and D, and the diagonals as AP and BQ.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 2. The area of the trapezium can be calculated as the absolute value of the difference between the height and the projection of the sum of the parallel sides on the longer base:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ \text{Area} = |h_AD - 2h_AP - 2h_BP - h_BD| \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Since \(h\), \(h_BP\), and \(h_BD\) are perpendicular distances, and by the properties of areas, \(h_BP\) and \(h_BD\) are corresponding heights, and summing these with \(2 \cdot \text{height}\) of the shorter bases formed by the diagonals and external distances we get equal division of the area between the quadrilaterals.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Thus, the diagonals of any division of a trapezium form pairs of two triangles which have full equal area. Hence, for the same area, a diagonals division can always be performed in such a way that equal triangles are formed for both quadrilaterals.
