[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To solve this problem, we need to find integer solutions \( n_1 \) and \( n_2 \) to the equation \( P(n) = n + 3 \).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Given:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m P(17) = 10 \quad \text{and} \quad P(24) = 17
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Since \( P(n_1) \) and \( P(n_2) \) are both solutions to the equation \( P(n) = n + 3 \):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m P(n_1) = n_1 + 3 \quad \text{and} \quad P(n_2) = n_2 + 3
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Thus, we can equate \( P(n) = n_1 + 3 \) and \( P(n) = n_2 + 3 \):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m n_1 + 3 = n_1 + 3 \quad \text{(which is true for all } n_1\text{)}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m n_2 + 3 = n_2 + 3
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Both equations are true, so they do not provide new information about \( n_1 \) and \( n_2 \) apart from their integer solutions. However, we need specific values for \( n_1 \) and \( n_2 \).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To find \( n_1 \) and \( n_2 \), we use the fact that:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m n_1 = n + 3 \quad \text{or} \quad n_2 = n + 3
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m For each of these expressions, we need to find integers \( n \) such that the polynomial satisfies the given conditions.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 1. For \( n_1 = n + 3 \):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m P(n_1) = P(n + 3)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Given \( P(17) = 10 \):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m P(17) = P(4 + 3) = 4P(3) = 4(10) = 40
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Thus:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m P(n) = n + 3
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m P(17) = 40 \quad \text{(which is consistent given intermediate terms)}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Clearly, there is no contradiction or inconsistency with \( P(n) = n + 3 \) unless it is the final consistent equation. Next, comparing with the \( P(n_2) = n_2 + 3 \):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m P(n_2) = P(24) = 27
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Thus, \( n_2 = n + 3 \) must also satisfy \( 27 \).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 2. For \( n_2 = n + 3 \):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m n_2 + 3 = (n + 3) + 3
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m n_2 + 3 = n + 6
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Solving for \( n \):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m n_2 + 3 = n + 6 \implies n_2 = n + 6 - 3 \implies n_2 = n + 3
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m This confirms that \( n_2 = n + 3 \) is consistent with our earlier results.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Finally, the problem asks us to find \( n_1 \) and \( n_2 \). Since:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m n_1 = n + 3 \quad \text{and} \quad n_2 = n + 3
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m We see that \( n_1 \) and \( n_2 \) must be considered distinct per distinct integer solutions \( x_1, x_2 \) to the quadratic equation \( x^2 - x - 3 = 0 \) when \( P(n_1) \) and \( P(n_2) \) within the range of polynomial coefficients space guarantee distinct solution pairs.
