[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m A circle is centered at $O$, $\overline{AB}$ is a diameter and $C$ is a point on the circle with $\angle COB = 50^\circ$.  What is the degree measure of $\angle CAB$?
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To find the degree measure of \(\angle CAB\), we will use the properties of a circle and the given angle.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Given:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m - \(\overline{AB}\) is a diameter.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m - Point \(C\) lies on the circle (\(\angle COB = 50^\circ\)) and \(\angle CAB\) will consequently be the complement of \(\angle COB\), since \(\angle COB\) is a straight angle (\(180^\circ\)).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \angle OCT = 180^\circ
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Thus, \(\angle OCT = 180^\circ\).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Next, since \(\angle COB\) is a straight angle, we know that:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \angle COB = 50^\circ
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Because \(\angle COB\) is a straight angle, the angle at the circumference that subtends this angle (which is \(\angle COF\)):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \angle COF = 180^\circ - 50^\circ = 130^\circ
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m In the coplanar circle of \(\angle COB\) and \(\angle COF\), the sum of the two inscribed angles is equal to three times the measure of an inscribed angle that subtends the same arc as both angles’ arcs:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \angle OBC + \angle CAB = 3 \times \angle COF = 3 \times 130^\circ = 390^\circ
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Changed to:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \angle OBC + \angle CAB = 180^\circ + \angle COF = 180^\circ + 130^\circ = 310^\circ
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m So, the degree measures of \(\angle OBC\) and \(\angle CAB\) (as well as vice versa) must be equal in degrees. Hence, \(\angle CAB = \angle OBC\):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \angle OBC = 310^\circ
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \boxed{310}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
