[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To find the largest integer less than 1000 that is divisible by 11, we need to compare each number with 1000 and find the largest number that satisfies this condition.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 1. Identify the largest number less than 1000 that is divisible by 11. We can start by checking the number 1000 itself, which is divisible by 11 twice (10 and 20) and 1000 again.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 2. Next, check for any larger numbers that satisfy the divisibility by 11. We can start by checking 999. That is divisible by 11 once (9 and 11) and 999 again.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 3. We can continue this process, but we need to be mindful of the fact that the larger the number, the larger its remainder when divided by 11, and thus less possible to be divisible by 11.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 4. To find the largest possible number, we need to reduce 999 further by chance to \( 999 \mod 11 = 0 \), making it 999 that exits 11. We can then compare 999 to 1000:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m     - Check \( 999 \mod 11 = 0 \). It's 11, less than 1000.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m     - |
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 5. Thus, we can reach our upper bound with 999 and move on to check 998. Check 1000 \div 11 gives a remainder of 9, so \( 999 + 9 = 918 \), then \( 918 + 9 = 927 \), then \( 927 + 9 = 936 \), then \( 936 + 9 = 945 \). 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m     So, 999, 927, 936, and 945 are all divisible by 11.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Therefore, the largest integer between 1000 and \( 1000 + 9 \) which is divisible by 11 is 998.
