[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m A stick 5 cm long, a stick 9 cm long, and a third stick $n$ cm long form a triangle. What is the sum of all possible whole number values of $n$?
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To find the sum of all possible whole number values of \( n \) for which three sticks \( 5 \) cm long, \( 9 \) cm long, and \( n \) cm long can form a triangle, we need to use the triangle inequality theorem. The theorem states that for any triangle, the sum of the lengths of any two sides must be greater than the length of the remaining side. Therefore, we have three conditions:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 1. \( 5 + 9 > n \)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 2. \( 5 + n > 9 \)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 3. \( 9 + n > 5 \)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Let's solve each inequality step by step:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 1. \( 5 + 9 > n \)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    14 > n \quad \text{or} \quad n < 14
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 2. \( 5 + n > 9 \)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    n > 9 - 5 \quad \text{or} \quad n > 4
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 3. \( 9 + n > 5 \)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    n > 5 - 9 \quad \text{or} \quad n > -4
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    Since \( n \) is a whole number, this inequality is always true and does not contribute to the sum of possible values of \( n \).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Combining the valid inequalities from the third scenario, we get:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 4 < n < 14 \quad \text{or} \quad n \in \{6, 7, 8, 9, 10, 11, 12, 13\}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Now, we sum these values:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 6 + 7 + 8 + 9 + 10 + 11 + 12 + 13
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m We can add these numbers step by step:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 6 + 7 = 13
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 13 + 8 = 21
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 21 + 9 = 30
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 30 + 10 = 40
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 40 + 11 = 51
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 51 + 12 = 63
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 63 + 13 = 76
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Thus, the sum of all possible whole number values of \( n \) is \(\boxed{76}\).<|endoftext|>
