[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To find the smallest positive integer \( x \) which is greater than 1 and relatively prime to 120, we need to check the GCD of \( x \) and 120. The smallest such \( x \) greater than 1 will satisfy this condition.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m First, let's determine the prime factors of 120:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 120 = 2^3 \times 3 \times 5
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To find the GCD of any number with this factorization, we need to consider all the common prime factors and the smallest power (which will always be 1).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m The prime factors of 120 are \( 2, 3, \) and \( 5 \). The factors of 120 are:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 10, 12, 15, 20, 24, 30, 40, 60, 120
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To find the GCD of any number with 120:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \text{GCD} = \max\{ x \mid 2^a \times 3^b \times 5^c \le x < 120 \}, \quad a, b, c \text{ are non-negative integers}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Since we want the smallest \( x \), we will start checking from \( x = 2 \) and go upwards until we find a number that is relatively prime to 120.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Let's test \( x = 2 \):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 2 \quad \text{(since } 2 \text{ has no factors of other primes) and GCD(2, 120) = 1, so this is relatively prime to 120)}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Thus, the smallest positive integer \( x \) which is greater than 1 and relatively prime to 120 is:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \boxed{2}<|endoftext|>
