[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m What is the remainder when $13^{13} +5$ is divided by 6?
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To find the remainder when $13^{13} + 5$ is divided by 6, we only need to consider the last two digits of the power of 13, since any number mod 10 results in the same remainder for any number $r$ that is less than that number (using modular arithmetic rules).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m The number $13$ ends in a 3, so we only need to consider $13^{13}$ modulo 6 to find the remainder for any integer $r$ from 0 to 5.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $$13^{13} \mod 6 = 13^3 \mod 6$$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m We can calculate $13^3$ modulo 6 as:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $$13^3 = 2197$$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Now, we find the remainder when 2197 is divided by 6:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $$2197 \div 6 = 366 \text{ remainder } 1 \Rightarrow 2197 \mod 6 = 1$$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m So, the remainder when $13^{13}$ is divided by 6 is $\boxed{1}$.<|endoftext|>
