[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m The method that I found suggested a better way but seems to reference prior knowledge that I haven't. I have the following as an example.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Take 5. It is rounded up and (plus) rounds down for 5, then 6, and so on. Likewise, 7 rounds up, 8 rounds down, and so forth. We then add up the results. The only two that round down are 45 and 48, because they need to fall between 4 and 5. The rest round up. We have eighths and a thousand, so a quesiton is: Why? Or am I looking for explanation of what is going on? Just for my own curiosity:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m “The method that I found suggested better way is the thing you must see a problem as a balanced sum. Quality is not Out / Len.” 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m This probably is not the right terminology. I am hoping to understand what impacts and is of use in terms of finding a correct method.  Technely I answered my own question in case it can be re pleiadified.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m As you noted, we say that $5$ rounds up, $6$ rounds down, $7$ rounds up, and so on.  So, $5+6+7+8+9=35$ for the sum of the first $5$ multiples of $5$.  There are indeed exactly $3$ numbers in this set.  It’s not a coincidence that they round to things like $35$ or $36$.  The point of this is that the pigeonhole effect for the order $5,7,8$ gives further clues as to a better calculation.  You don’t have $9$ or $10$ or more, so if we applied a decrement, understanding that we are working modulo $5$, we could deduce that the entire sum would be $3$ more than $5$.  Similarly, if we were working in modulo $6$ by deручing the first result, we would deduce that the sum would be $3$ more than $4$.  The $9$ sixth results would be the same just in a different order.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m So, to solve the problem $
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 5+\; 6+\; 7+\; 8+\; 9 + 10
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $you could check the numbering of the multiples, so $5,7,8,9,10$.  Calculate the sum of these which are the $5$ whole numbers.  Then, add the remaining number $1$ to the $10$ to give the combined $11$ numbers.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Each of order $0,1,3,6$.  I speculate students often default to the first addition as "About three more than the first number plus the next", however, this is understand by considering the parity of the sum.  Again most students do not think about the order of the factors of the sum. Another common mistake is to not apply modular arithmetic to the remainder of the sum.  For instance, $3+5+7+8+10+12 =17,000$ modulo $6$ is $3+5+7+5+9=26$ which is $4$ less than $17,000$, which solves the problem, but taking the help of any other well known fact is more than $3$.  This process has found many yet is still emerging as students start to see more oddities in the ordering, like having consecutive orders that fall between $9$ and $10$ with an additional following $1$, where some developers have defined such as "skip+"-something like those of similar nature (e.g. $50$ to $65$ or $100$ to $120$, or $0\frac12+ 1$)  Our work fine constructing such loops.  Another puzzling structure is that consecutive subsequent numbers (e.g. $9\ 10$ or $14\ 15$ with $15$ under 10) to aid understand the mapping $\modulo 3 and 5$ is not aligned to other such examples.  Note, many other mixed order sets exist but I do find similar numbers all around the figure 21 at the bottom of this forum.  When $x\in [0,m]$, we know that
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $$-x+m \le x\le x+m$$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m for  $x\in (-\infty,0]$.  Same is true for $\infty\le x\le\infty$.  Binomial theorem can be thought of showing the $m$th term is zero; however, it is similar but not the same fact:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $$\binom\alpha{0+1+12}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m =m!\sum_{k=0}^3\binom{4}{k}(-1)^{4-k}\cdot\frac{1}{x^k\cdot4^k}\cdot(x+k)^{4-k}$$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m And here, we are summing over smaller binomials that are indexed by the same index, so we have removed the $(-1)^4$ from the equation of $(2,0)$, making the $4$th term completely null as sets 2 and 4 are different orderings.
