[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Of the 50 students in the drama club, 36 take mathematics, 27 take physics and 20 students take both mathematics and physics. How many drama club students take neither mathematics nor physics?
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Sure. Let's use set notation to define the variables and then calculate the answer using set theory.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Let
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \begin{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m N &= \text{Total number of drama club students} \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m M &= \text{Number of students who take mathematics} \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m P &= \text{Number of students who take physics} \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m T &= \text{Number of students who take both mathematics and physics} \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m N^c &= \text{Number of students who do not take either mathematics nor physics} \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m N^c &\subset M \cup P \cup M \cap P \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m N^c &\subset (M \cup P \cup M \cap P)^\complement \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m N^c &= (M \cup P \cup M \cap P)^\complement \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \mathcal B &= M \cup P \cup (M \cap P) \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m B &= M \cup P \cup M \cap P \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m N^c &= M \cap P \cup (M \cap P)^\complement \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \end{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m We also know that
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $$\mathcal B = M \cup P \cup M \cap P = (M \cup P) \cup (M \cap P)$$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Since M and P are disjoint, we have that
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $$|B| = |(M \cup P) \cup (M \cap P)|$$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $$\implies |B| = 2 \times 15$$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m We would like to find the maximum number of elements in N^c, so of course we should count them up from the following:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $$N^c = (M \cap P) \cup (M \cap P)^\complement = |M \cap P| + |(M \cap P)^\complement|$$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m and since we do not know what M ∩ P = we can also think about that as:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $$N^c = |M| + |P| - |M \cap P| - |M \cap P|^\complement$$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Thus we simply count them for (1 + 20) - 36 - 27 = 33
