[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To find out how many perfect squares lie between 50 and 250, we can first find out how many perfect squares lie between 1 and 50. The smallest integer whose square is greater than 50 is \(\sqrt{50}\) and the largest integer whose square is less than 50 is \(\sqrt{50}-1\). So, in this case, within the range 1 to 50, there are 7 perfect squares (from \(7^2 = 49\) to \(8^2 = 64\)).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Next, to find out how many perfect squares lie between 50 and 250, we need to identify how many perfect squares lie between these two numbers. 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m The smallest such integer whose square is greater than 250 is \(\lceil \sqrt{250} \rceil = 16\) (since \(16^2 = 256\) and \(17^2 = 289\)), and the largest such integer is \(\lfloor \sqrt{250} \rfloor - 1 = 14\). So the range covered by our perfect squares from 50 to 250 is from 49 to 64.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m The perfect squares from 49, 64, 100, 176, 192, 225, and 256 fall within our range (1 to 250). So, there are 7 perfect squares that lie between 50 and 250. 
