[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m i^5 &= i \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m i^6 &= -1 \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m i^7 &= -i \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m i^8 &= 1 \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \end{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m From this, we can see the cycle repeat every 4 terms. Therefore, to find \(i^n\), we compute the remainder when \(n\) is divided by 4:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 4k + r \quad \text{where} \quad k \in \mathbb{Z} \quad \text{and} \quad 0 \leq r < 4
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Let’s apply this to \(n = 14762\), \(n = 14763\), \(n = 14764\), and \(n = 14765\):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 14762 \div 4 = 3690 \text{ remainder } 2 \quad \Rightarrow \quad i^{14762} \equiv i^2 \pmod{5}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 14763 \div 4 = 3690 \text{ remainder } 3 \quad \Rightarrow \quad i^{14763} \equiv i^3 \pmod{5}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 14764 \div 4 = 3691 \text{ remainder } 2 \quad \Rightarrow \quad i^{14764} \equiv i^2 \pmod{5}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 14765 \div 4 = 3691 \text{ remainder } 1 \quad \Rightarrow \quad i^{14765} \equiv i^1 \pmod{5}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Now that we have computed these equivalences within the natural numbers (i.e., \(0 \leq r < 4\)), we can evaluate the original expression:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m i^{14762} + i^{14763} + i^{14764} + i^{14765} = i^2 + i^3 + i^2 + i^1
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Substituting back using the cyclical nature:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m i^2 = -1\quad \text{and} \quad i^3 = -i
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Substitute these values into the expression:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m -1 + (-i) + (-1) + i
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Combine the terms:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m -1 - 1 + (-i) + i = -2
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Thus, the final result in modular arithmetic is:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m -\boxed{2}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
