[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m How many degrees are in the measure of the smaller angle that is formed by the hour-hand and minute-hand of a clock when it is 5 o'clock?
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To find the angle, we need to determine how many degrees each hour hand moves. A full circle is $360$ degrees.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m The hour hand moves in a circle that has $12$ equal graduations (degrees), so in one hour, it will rotate $30$ degrees.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m The minute hand moves in a clockwise direction, rotating $60$ degrees every minute.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m At $5 o'clock$, the hour hand would be exactly at the 5 and the minute hand would be at the 12. 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Since the vertical hand difference is $5 - 1 = 4$ degrees, the smaller angle formed lies between the hour hand and the minute hand. To find this angle, we subtract the vertical hand difference from one full rotation:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $$30 \text{ degrees} - 4 \text{ degrees} = 26 \text{ degrees}$$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m So, at $5 o'clock$, the measure of the smaller angle formed by the hour hand and the minute hand is $\boxed{26}$ degrees.<|endoftext|>
