[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m According to the expression, we multiply each term by the variable to expand it. Start with \(x^4\):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ (5)^4 = 625 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Next, multiply \(x y^3\):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ (5 \cdot 4)^3 = 20^3 = 8000 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Inside the parentheses, \(x^3 y^2\) needs to be expanded since the variables are in exponents. First, we multiply \(x\) and \(y\):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ (5 \cdot 4) \cdot y = 20y \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Now, we square \(x\):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ x^3 = (5 \cdot 4)^3 = 20^3 = 8000 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Putting all the other terms together, we have:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ 625 + 8000 + 8000 + 20y + y^3 + \text{lower degree terms} = 8115 + 20y + y^3 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Now, substituting \(x = 5\) and \(y = 4\):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ 8115 + 20(4) + 4^3 = 8115 + 80 + 64 = 8299 \]
