[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Step 1: Find the intersection of the line with the $x$-axis.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m When $y=0$, we substitute $y=0$ into the line equation and solve for $x$:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \begin{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 3(0)-4x & = 12 \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m -4x & = 12 \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m x & = \frac{12}{-4} \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m x & = -3
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \end{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Step 2: Verify if the $x$-coordinate is non-negative.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Since $x = -3$ is a negative number, it does not satisfy the original condition where we want the $x$-coordinate to be non-negative.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Thus, the line $\boxed{-3, y=0}$ intersects the $x$-axis at point $\boxed{-3}$.
