[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Given the hyperbola $\dfrac{x^{2}}{a^{2}}-\dfrac{y^{2}}{b^{2}}=1 (a > 0, b > 0)$ with left and right foci $F_{1}$ and $F_{2}$ respectively. A straight line $l$ with slope $1$ passes through $F_{1}$ and intersects the hyperbola at points $A$ and $B$, such that $\overrightarrow{AF_{1}}=2\overrightarrow{F_{1}A}$, and the area of $\triangle ABF_{2}$ is equal to half the area of the hyperbola. If the eccentricity of the hyperbola is $\dfrac{\sqrt{6}}{2}$, find $|AB|$. To solve this problem, we will use the properties of hyperbolas and vector calculations. The equation of the hyperbola is $\frac{x^2}{a^2} - \frac{y^2}{b^2} = 1$ with $a > 0$, $b > 0$.
