[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m What is the slope of the line containing the midpoint of the segment with endpoints at (0, 0) and (2, 3) and the midpoint of the segment with endpoints at (5, 0) and (6, 3)? Express your answer in simplest form.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Step 1: First, we calculate the midpoints of both segments.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Midpoint of (0, 0) to (2, 3) = ((0 + 2)/2, (0 + 3)/2) = (1, 1.5)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Midpoint of (5, 0) to (6, 3) = ((5 + 6)/2, (0 + 3)/2) = (5.5, 1.5)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Step 2: Calculate the slope of each segment.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Slope of the line segment with endpoints (0, 0) to (2, 3) = (3-0)/(2-0) = 3/2
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Slope of the line segment with endpoints (5, 0) to (6, 3) = (3-0)/(6-5) = 3/1 = 3
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Step 3: Subtract the slope of the first segment from the slope of the second segment to find the slope of the line containing the midpoints.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Slope = 3/1 - 3/2 = 6/2 - 3/2 = 3/2
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
