[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m What is the greatest prime factor of 3105?
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To find the greatest prime factor of 3105, we first factorize 3105 into its prime factors.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m We start by dividing 3105 by the smallest prime number, which is 3.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[3105 \div 3 = 1035\]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m So, \(3105 = 3 \times 1035\)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Next, we factorize 1035.  
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m The smallest prime number greater than 1035 is 103.  
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[1035 \div 103 = 10\]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m So, \(1035 = 103 \times 10\)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m And finally, divide 10 by 3:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[10 = 3 \times 10 \div 3 = 3 \times 3 \times 5 = 3^3 \times 5\]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Putting all the prime factors together, we get:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[3105 = 3 \times 3 \times 3 \times 5 \times 103\]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m So, the prime factors of 3105 are \(3, 3, 5, 103\).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m The greatest prime factor among these is \(335\), because it appears the highest power of each of \(3, 5,\) and \(103\) that divides into 3105.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Therefore, the greatest prime factor of \(3105\) is \(\boxed{335}\). 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
