[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To do the problem step by step, first we need to find the interior angle of a regular hexagon. One interior angle of a regular polygon can be determined by subtracting \(180\) degrees from any \(360\) degrees. Thus, the interior angle of the hexagon is given by:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \text{Interior angle} = 360^\circ - 360^\circ \div 6 = 360^\circ - 60^\circ = 300^\circ
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Now, to find the degree of each external angle of the hexagon, recall that the sum of the exterior angles of any polygon (and in this case, a hexagon) is always \(360\) degrees. Each exterior angle is formed by two adjacent sides of the polygon, effectively forming an angle with one side. In the case of a regular hexagon:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \text{Each external angle} = \text{Sum of exterior angles} \div \text{Number of sides} = \frac{360}{6} = 60^\circ
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m By recognizing the relationship between the interior angle and the external angle of a regular hexagon:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \text{Each external angle} = 180 \div \text{Number of sides} = \frac{360}{6} = 60^\circ
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m It is crucial to note that this relationship holds true for all polygons, and in this context, it is perfectly correct. Thus, the degree value of each external angle of the hexagon is \(60^\circ\). Keep in mind that the uniqueness of the degree of each external angle in a regular hexagon is inherently self-contained given the geometry of the hexagon, and confirming values for each interior or external angle from basic geometric principles or specific calculations for certain polygons is a valid source of information. The escalated answer is:
