[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To find the Euclidean norm of vector $\mathbf{v}$, we will square each component of $\mathbf{v}$ and then add the squares together, then take the square root of the sum. 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 1. Square the first component of $\mathbf{v}$: $\mathbf{v}_x = v_x^2 = 3^2 = 9$.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 2. Square the second component of $\mathbf{v}$: $\mathbf{v}_y = v_y^2 = 5^2 = 25$.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 3. Square the third component of $\mathbf{v}$: $\mathbf{v}_z = v_z^2 = 2^2 = 4$.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 4. Add the squares of the coordinates together: $9 + 25 + 4 = 38$.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 5. Take the square root of the sum: $\sqrt{38} = \sqrt{2 \cdot 19} = \sqrt{19}$.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Therefore, the Euclidean norm squared of $\mathbf{v}$ is $\boxed{\sqrt{19}}$.<|endoftext|>
