[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To find the smallest two-digit prime number that is NOT an emirp, we need to identify the prime numbers within the range of 10 to 99 and check if their digits, when reversed, result in a prime number.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Let's start by listing the prime numbers between 10 and 99 and reversing their digits:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 10 is not prime.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 11 -> 1 -> 1 is prime.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 12 -> 2 -> 2 is prime.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m ...
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 89 -> 9 -> 9 is not prime.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m So, the emirp primes are 11 and 37. Therefore, the numbers 10 and 89 are not emirps. The smallest two-digit prime number is 11.
