[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To evaluate the expression \(i^{22} + i^{222}\), we need to understand the behavior of the powers of \(i\). The powers of \(i\) cycle every four powers:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \begin{aligned}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m i^1 &= i \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m i^4 &= 1 \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m i^7 &= i^2 \cdot i^3 = -1 \cdot (-i) = i \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m i^10 &= i^2 \cdot i^8 = -1 \cdot 1 = -1 \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m i^{13} &= i^{2 \cdot 6 + 1} = i^1 = i \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m i^{16} &= i^{2 \cdot 8 + 0} = i^0 = 1 \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \end{aligned}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m This pattern repeats every four powers. So, calculating \(i^{22}\) and \(i^{222}\):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \begin{aligned}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m i^{22} &= i^{2 \cdot 11 + 2} = (i^4)^{11} \cdot i^2 = 1^{11} \cdot i^2 = i^2 = -1 \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m i^{222} &= i^{2 \cdot 111 + 0} = i^{0} = 1
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \end{aligned}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Adding these results together:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m i^{22} + i^{222} = -1 + 1 = 0
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Therefore, the final answer is:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \boxed{0}
