[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m How many ordered pairs of real numbers $(x,y)$ satisfy the following system of equations? \[\left\{ \begin{aligned} x+3y&=3 \\ \left| |x| - |y| \right| &= 1 \end{aligned}\right.\]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To solve the given system of equations \(\left\{ \begin{aligned} x + 3y & = 3 \\ \left| |\sqrt{3 - x} + 1 \right| &= 1 \end{aligned}\right.\), we need to analyze it step by step.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m ### Step 1: Analyze the second equation
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m The second equation is \(\left| |\sqrt{3 - x} + 1 \right| = 1\). This equation means that the expression inside the absolute value can be either 1 or -1. This gives us two possible cases:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ |\sqrt{3 - x} + 1| = 1 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m ### Case 1: \(\sqrt{3 - x} + 1 = 1\)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Thus, we solve:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ \sqrt{3 - x} = 0 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ 3 - x = 0 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ x = 3 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m ### Case 2: \(\sqrt{3 - x} + 1 = -1\)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Thus, we solve:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ \sqrt{3 - x} = -2 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Since the square root of a real number cannot be negative:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ \sqrt{3 - x} = -2 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m This set of equations \(\left\{ \begin{aligned} x = 3 \\ \sqrt{3} - |y| = 1 \end{aligned}\right.\) is not valid because it leads to \(\sqrt{3} < 0\), which is not possible.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m ### Consistency Check
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m We must check if both cases are consistent and correct.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m #### Case 1: \(x = 3\)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Substitute \(x = 3\) into the first equation:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ 3 + 3y = 3 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ 3y = 0 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ y = 0 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m So, \((x, y) = (3, 0)\) satisfies both equations.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m #### Case 2: Check possible \(\frac{\sqrt{3} - |y|}{3}\)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m For \(\frac{\sqrt{3} - |y|}{3}\) to be a valid real number:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ 3 > \sqrt{3} - |y| \geq 1 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m We test:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ y = 0 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ 3 > \sqrt{3} - |0| = 3 \] (False)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Thus, \(y = 0\) is not a valid solution.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m ### Verification
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Since the only consistent solution is \((x, y) = (3, 0)\), the number of ordered pairs of real numbers \((x, y)\) that satisfy the system is:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \boxed{1}
