[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To solve the problem, we need to determine how many of the smallest 200 positive integers are congruent to \(1 \mod 9\).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m First, let's identify the smallest positive integer that satisfies this property. The smallest positive integer \(n\) is \(1\), since \(0 \mod 9 = 0 \neq 1\) and any positive integer \(n\) will be congruent to \(1 \mod 9\) because \(0 \leq n < 9\) holds true for all positive integers \(n\).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m So, we only need to consider the smallest 200 positive integers that start with 1.
