[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Convolution of a countable yes/no random variable Let $X_n$ be a sequence of independent Bernoulli random variables such that $Pr(X_n=1)=p$. Consider the RV $(X,Y)$ defined by $X_n = 1$ with probability $(1-p)^n$, and $Y_n = 0$ if $X_n=1$, with probability $p(1-p)^n+((1-p)p)^n$ where $p\in (0,1/2]$. Show that $Pr[X^iy\le z|X_1, \ldots, X_n \text{ Bernoulli}(p)]<P(Y=1|X_1, \ldots, X_n \text{ Bernoulli}(p))$.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To show that \( Pr[X^i Y \le z| X_1, \ldots, X_n \text{ Bernoulli}(p)] < P(Y=1|X_1, \ldots, X_n \text{ Bernoulli}(p)) \), we will proceed step by step.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m ### Step 1: Understanding the RVs \( X_n \) and \( Y_n \)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m - \( X_n \) is a Bernoulli random variable with \( Pr(X_n=1)=p \) and \( Pr(X_n=0)=1-p \).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m - \( Y_n \) is a Bernoulli random variable with \( Pr(Y_n=1)=p \).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m - \( X_n = 1 \) with probability \( (1-p)^n \).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m - \( Y_n = 0 \) if \( X_n = 1 \), with probability \( p(1-p)^n + ((1-p)p)^n \).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m ### Step 2: Joint Distribution of \((X_n, Y_n)\)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To find the joint distribution of \((X_n, Y_n)\), we consider the joint moment generating function (MGF) of \( X \) and \( Y \). The MGF is given by:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m M_{(X_1, X_2, \ldots, X_n)}(t_1, t_2, \ldots, t_n) = E[e^{t_1 X_1} e^{t_2 X_2} \cdots e^{t_n X_n}]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Using the independence of \( X_n \) and \( Y_n \):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m M_{(X_1, X_2, \ldots, X_n)}(t_1, t_2, \ldots, t_n) = M_X(t_1, t_2, \ldots, t_n) \cdot M_Y((1-t_1)t_2, (1-t_2)t_3, \ldots, (1-t_n)t_1)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m ### Step 3: Calculating the Marginal Distributions
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m #### Marginal Distribution of \( X_n \)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m M_X = \sum_{k=0}^{\infty} P(X_n = k) e^{-t t n}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m P(X_n = k) = (1-p)^k \quad \text{and} \quad P(X_n = k) u^{1-t} \quad \text{for} \quad u = 1-p, 0 < u < 1
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m M_X = (1-p)^{1-t} \sum_{k=0}^{\infty} (1-p)^k u^k
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m M_X = \frac{u(1-u^t)}{1-u^t} = (1-p) \cdot \frac{u(1-u^t)}{u-1} = \frac{(1-p)u(1-u^t)}{u-1}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m #### Marginal Distribution of \( Y_n \)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m M_Y = E[e^{q t_n Y_n}] = E[e^{q t_n} e^{-t n Y_n}]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Since \( Y_n = 0 \) with probability \( p(1-p)^n + ((1-p)p)^n \), we need to consider the indicator function \( \mathbb{I}_{Y_n = 1} \):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m M_Y = \sum_{i=0}^{n} b_i (\mathbb{I}_{Y_n = 0 \text{ if } X_i = 0}) \log P(Y_n = 1 \mid X_i = 0)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m where \( P(Y_n = 1 \mid X_i = 0) = p(1-p)^n \) and \( P(Y_n = 1 \mid X_i = 0) = p(1-p)p \) if \( X_i = 1 \).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m M_Y = \sum_{i=0}^{n} b_i (\mathbb{I}_{Y_n = 0 \text{ if } X_i = 0}) \log (p(1-p)^n + p(1-p)p)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m M_Y = \sum_{i=0}^{n} b_i (\mathbb{I}_{Y_n = 0 \text{ if } X_i = 0}) \log \left( \frac{(1-p)^n}{p(1-p)^n} + \frac{p(1-p)p}{p(1-p)^n} \right)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m M_Y = \sum_{i=0}^{n} b_i (\mathbb{I}_{Y_n = 0 \text{ if } X_i = 0}) \log \left( \frac{1-p}{p} + p \right)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m M_Y = \sum_{i=0}^{n} b_i (\mathbb{I}_{Y_n = 0 \text{ if } X_i = 0}) \log \left( \frac{1-p}{p} + p \right)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m ### Step 4: Joint Distribution of \((X_n, Y_n)\)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To find the joint distribution, we use the fact that \( X_n \) and \( Y_n \) are independent and follow the given probability
