[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Diane decrees that she can skulk her teeth out during the Tri-X commercial, and she does this with each passing minute, and every post on FBscreen she will constantly look at the flowers, however the amount of time per post increases linearly. Diane posts 8 times in minutes 2, 2.9, 4.4, 5.9, 7.4, 9.9, 11.4, and 12.9. Assume the above affects her posting. What is the net effect on the number of flowers she is able to look at, in terms of seconds? To determine the net effect on the number of flowers Diane is able to look at in terms of seconds, we need to analyze the effect of Diane's posting on the time she spends looking at the flowers. We will calculate the total amount of time spent looking at the flowers and then calculate the duration she can maintain this amount of time.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m First, let's identify the points of posting for each minute:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m - At 2 minutes, Diane posts once. She looks at flowers for 10 minutes (8 minutes spent looking at the flowers, 2 minutes not moving).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m - At 2.9 minutes, Diane posts twice. She looks at flowers for 8 minutes (7 minutes spent looking at the flowers, 2 minutes not moving).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m - At 4.4 minutes, Diane posts three times. Since she doesn't spend any time moving, she looks at flowers for 7 minutes (6 minutes spent looking at the flowers, 1 minute not moving).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m - At 5.9 minutes, Diane posts four times. Since she doesn't spend any time moving, she looks at flowers for 6 minutes (5 minutes spent looking at the flowers, 1 minute not moving).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m - At 7.4 minutes, Diane posts five times. Since she doesn't spend any time moving, she looks at flowers for 5 minutes (4 minutes spent looking at the flowers, 1 minute not moving).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m - At 9.9 minutes, Diane posts six times. Since she doesn't spend any time moving, she looks at flowers for 4 minutes (3 minutes spent looking at the flowers, 1 minute not moving).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m - At 11.4 minutes, Diane posts seven times. Since she doesn't spend any time moving, she looks at flowers for 3 minutes (2 minutes spent looking at the flowers, 1 minute not moving).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m - At 12.9 minutes, Diane posts eight times. Since she doesn't spend any time moving, she looks at flowers for 2 minutes (1 minute spent looking at the flowers).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Next, let's calculate the total minutes spent looking at the flowers:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m - At 2 minutes, 0 minutes
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m - At 2.9 minutes, 8 minutes
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m - At 4.4 minutes, 7 minutes
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m - At 5.9 minutes, 6 minutes
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m - At 7.4 minutes, 5 minutes
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m - At 9.9 minutes, 4 minutes
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m - At 11.4 minutes, 3 minutes
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m - At 12.9 minutes, 2 minutes
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m The total time Diane spends looking at the flowers is:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[0 + 8 + 7 + 6 + 5 + 4 + 3 + 2 = 33 \text{ minutes}\]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Assuming the above affects her posting, and because Diane is posting continuously without any breaks, the duration she maintains is the same as the total time she spends looking at the flowers. Therefore, the net effect on the number of flowers she is able to look at is:
