[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To find the area of the shaded region, we need to understand the geometric arrangement described. Here's a step-by-step breakdown:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 1. **Identify the shapes and their positions:**
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    - The outer square has a side length of 13 inches.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    - The middle square has a side length of 9 inches (half the side length of the outer square).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 2. **Determine the positions of the shaded region:**
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    - The shaded region is a triangle formed by the intersection of the extension of AD (from \(A\) to \(F\)) with the extensions of GC (from \(G\) to \(H\)) and BH.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 3. **Visualize the positions of the points:**
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    - Triangle \(ABF\) is part of the outer square.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    - Triangle \(CDE\) is part of the middle square.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 4. **Determine the area of each triangle:**
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    - Since the triangles share the LC side (which is 3 inches because the x-axis bisects the arcs at \(A\) and \(F\)), we can use the formula for the area of a triangle with given angle:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m      \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m      \text{Area} = \frac{1}{2} \times \text{base} \times \text{height}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m      \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    - For triangle \(ABF\) with base \(AF\) (which is a side of the outer square): 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m      \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m      \text{Area} = \frac{1}{2} \times 10 \times \text{height of } ABF
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m      \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m      Since \(AF = 13\):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m      \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m      \text{Area of } ABF = \frac{1}{2} \times 10 \times 13 = 65 \text{ square inches}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m      \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 5. **Determine the range of heights for triangles \(CDE\) and \(CDE\) (`DC'` and `DE`):**
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    - For the triangle \(CDE\) with \(C = (10, 3)\) and \(D = (13, 3)\):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m      \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m      \text{Height of } CDE = DC' + DC = HA + HC = 9 + 5 = 14 \text{ inches}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m      \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    - For the right triangle \(CDE\) with \(CDE = 14\) square inches:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m      \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m      \text{Height of } CDE = 10 - 6 = 4 \text{ square inches}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m      \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 6. **Compute the area of the shaded region:**
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    - The shaded region is the area of triangle \(CDE\) minus the area of the rectangle \(BCH\) minus the area of the rectangle \(OPENG\):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m      \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m      \text{Shaded Area} = 4 \text{ square inches} - 10 \times 9 - 65 \text{ square inches}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m      \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 7. **Calculate the area of the rectangle \(OPENG\):**
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    - Since \(OP = OB = 3\) inches (length of the outer square):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m      \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m      \text{Area of } OPG = 3 \times 3 = 9 \text{ square inches}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m      \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 8. **Subtract the area of rectangles \(BCH\) and \(OPENG\):**
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    \text{Shaded Area} = 4 - 9 - 65 = 37 \text{ square inches}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    \]
