[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m If the point $(x,y)$ is randomly chosen from inside the rectangle with vertices $(0,0)$, $(4,0)$, $(4,1)$, and $(0,1)$, then the area of the rectangle is $4 \times 1 = 4$.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m The area inside the rectangular portion is the area of the rectangle minus the area of the horizontal and vertical lines that make up the rectangle. 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Since $(x,y)$ can range from $0$ to $1$, the point can only achieve two values. There is no overlap of the horizontal lines which would overlap the point, nor the vertical lines, which would overlap the point.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Therefore, the portion of the area that lies inside the rectangle and the portion that lies outside this rectangle is simply equal. 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m So we are trying to find the probability that a random point will lie within the rectangle, not even given that it doesn't lie within any of the horizontal and vertical lines defined by those rectangles.
