[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Find the matrix that corresponds to rotating about the origin by an angle of $45^\circ$ clockwise.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m This problem requires us to find the matrix that represents the rotation of a point $(a, b)$ in an $x$-$y$ plane by an angle $\theta$ around the origin. The rotation matrix has the form:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \begin{bmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m c & -s \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m s & c
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \end{bmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m where $c = \cos(\theta)$ and $s = \sin(\theta)$.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Under the given condition of $45^\circ$, we have $\cos(45^\circ) = \sin(45^\circ) = \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}$. Thus, the rotation matrix simplifies to:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \begin{bmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \cos(45^\circ) & \sin(45^\circ) \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \sin(45^\circ) & \cos(45^\circ)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \end{bmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m =
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \begin{bmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} & \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} & \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \end{bmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Putting the final answer within $\boxed{\}$ notation:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \boxed{
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \begin{bmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} & \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} & \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \end{bmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m }
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m And here I explain how we could have reached this solution using Maple:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m We write $$\begin{equation*}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \matrix{
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m   &  & \text{WithOptions} & 1 & 0 & 0 \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m   & \text{or} & \text{Transpose to a column matrix} & 1 & -1 & 0 & \dots \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m   & \text{in that case we have elements } & 1 & -1 & 0 & \cdots & \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} \end{equation*}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m We'll examine Under the condition of Rotation ``$45^{\circ\xspace}$ equivalently $\theta = \frac{\pi}{4}$ in the $\left( x, y \right)$ plane $\:\begin{bmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 1 & \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} & -1
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \end{bmatrix}$.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Mathematica can do this on its own: 
