[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m A teacher wants to find out if there is a highly significant difference between boys and girls' test scores. What would be the issue in using a t-test? a . a gender variable (or its designation) is a population and the gender variable is the population parameter of interest. b. The variable whose levels are the population is the corresponding variable for the comparison. c. Both are objectively true. d. A participant's gender is at variance from his or her class level. e. A participant does not have more than one gender.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m The issue with using a t-test when comparing two small numbers of groups is that it assumes the data will come from a finite population of constant size. However, the strength of our claims about the difference are not guaranteed. This means that if the validity of two tests is hypothesis-testing the difference in stndar deviation, any effect of interest is not necessarily propagated to the threshold specified by the t statistic. See below for a formal way to show what will or will not happen in the above case.<|endoftext|>
