[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To find the greatest whole number less than 100 with an odd number of positive factors, we need to determine the largest integer that has no repeat factors (i.e., no repeated divisors). This challenge involves prime factorization being odd for these integers.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 1. **Prime Factorization:**
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    We start by expressing the number \( n \) as a product of prime numbers:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    n = p_1^{e_1} p_2^{e_2} \cdots p_k^{e_k}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    Here, \( p_1, p_2, \ldots, p_k \) are the prime factors of \( n \), and \( e_1, e_2, \ldots, e_k \) are their respective positions.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 2. **Finding Odd Total Factors:**
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    An integer \( n \) has an odd number of factors if and only if the exponents \( e_1, e_2, \ldots, e_k \) for each prime \( p_i \) are all either 0 or 1. This way, all groups of \( p_i \) that appear to an even power are canceled out.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 3. **My Selection:**
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    Among all possible factorizations of \( n \):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    - If all primes \( p_i \) contain 1, they were not prime.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    - If there are two 0's each, the resulting number is divisible by \( p \).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    - If there are two 0's and one \( p \), the resulting number is not divisible by \( p \).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 4. **Highest \( n \):**
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    - Isolate the factorization where all primes had 1.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    - Determine the largest possible \( n \) with the highest factorization.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Mathematically:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $$ n = p_1^{ex_1} p_2^{ex_2} \cdots p_k^{ex_k} $$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m We choose to isolate the choice where all primes have a 1, resulting in the largest possible \( n \):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m n = 1 \times 2 \times \ldots \times k \quad \text{(where n is prime)}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Maximize by choosing the prime factor whose exponents are as large as possible:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m n \text{ is prime and maximum value.}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Checking factorization
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m  - \( 16 = 2^4 \quad (hundreds digits are 16 = Total)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m  - \( 24 \in (8,15), (16,13), (17,11), (19,7), (23,6), (29,4), (31,3) \).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m   
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m The largest 15 decimal digit number less than 100.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \boxed{128}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m }
