[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Find the remainder when $x^{44} + x^{33} + x^{22} + x^{11} + 1$ is divided by $x^4 + x^3 + x^2 + x + 1.$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Certainly! We can solve this problem using the concept of polynomial roots and the properties of the polynomial itself.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m First, let’s identify the polynomial \(P(x) = x^{44} + x^{33} + x^{22} + x^{11} + 1\). Notice that \(P(x)\) is related to the polynomial \(Q(x) = x^{44} + x^{33} + x^{22} + x^{11} + 1\).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Since both polynomials share a common factor (i.e., \(x^{11}\) in both), we can factor out \(x^{11}\) first:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[P(x) = x^{44} + x^{33} + x^{22} + x^{11} + 1 = x^{11}(x^{39} + x^{38} + x^{37} + x^{36} + x^{30} + x^{10} + 1).\]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Our goal is to find the remainder when \(P(x)\) is divided by \(x^4 + x^3 + x^2 + x + 1\). For this, we can use polynomial division on \(P(x)\).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m By polynomial division method:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[x^{11}(x^3 + x^3 + x^3 + x^3 + x^3 + x^3 + x^3 + x^3 + x^3 + x^3 +x^3 + x+ \frac{1}{x^4+\cdots} ) =P(x).\]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[x^{10}\cdot(1+\frac{1}{x+1}) =0.\]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m So, the remainder when \(P(x)\) is $x^{10} \cdot(1+\frac{1}{x+1})$. Since when \(x^4 + x^3 + x^2 + x + 1 =p(1+x+1)=p\xrightarrow{p=-6}\) the expression is undefined. Therefore, the remainder value is zero:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[x^{10} \cdot 1 + 1 = 1.\]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Hence, the remainder when \(x^{44} + x^{33} + x^{22} + x^{11} + 1\) is divided by \(x^{44} +x^{33} + x^{22} + x^{11} +1\) is \(1\).
