[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Yes, we can create a proportion to find the width of the new rectangle.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Let's call the length of the new rectangle X and the width W. Since it is proportional to the length, the length will be 9 times the width.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m So, we have:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 6 * 9 = 10 * W
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Solving for W, we get:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 6W = 54
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m W = 54 / 6
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m W = 9 inches
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Therefore, the new rectangle has a width of 9 inches.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m ####
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To find the width of the new rectangle, we can set up a proportion:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 6 inches (length) x W (width) = 9 inches (new length)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Solving for W gives:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m W = (9 inches / 6 inches) x 9 inches
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m W = 9/6 x 9 inches
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m W = 9/6 x 9/1
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m W = 9 x 3 / 6
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m W = 27/6
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m W = 4.5 inches
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Therefore, the new rectangle is 4.5 inches wide.<|endoftext|>
