[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To find the $3 \times 3$ matrix $\mathbf{M}$ such that $\mathbf{M} \mathbf{v} = -4 \mathbf{v}$ for all three-dimensional vectors $\mathbf{v}$, we need to express the condition in terms of matrix equations.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Let's denote $\mathbf{v}$ as an $n \times 1$ vector, i.e., $\mathbf{v} = \begin{pmatrix} v_1 \\ v_2 \\ v_3 \end{pmatrix}$. The condition $\mathbf{M} \mathbf{v} = -4 \mathbf{v}$ can be written as:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \begin{pmatrix} M_{11} & M_{12} & M_{13} \\ M_{21} & M_{22} & M_{23} \\ M_{31} & M_{32} & M_{33} \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} v_1 \\ v_2 \\ v_3 \end{pmatrix} = -4 \begin{pmatrix} v_1 \\ v_2 \\ v_3 \end{pmatrix}.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m This gives us the following system of linear equations:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \begin{cases}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m M_{11} v_1 + M_{12} v_2 + M_{13} v_3 = -4 v_1, \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m M_{21} v_1 + M_{22} v_2 + M_{23} v_3 = -4 v_2, \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m M_{31} v_1 + M_{32} v_2 + M_{33} v_3 = -4 v_3.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \end{cases}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m We can represent this system in matrix form as $A \mathbf{v} = -4 \mathbf{v}$, where:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m A = \begin{pmatrix} M_{11} & M_{12} & M_{13} \\ M_{21} & M_{22} & M_{23} \\ M_{31} & M_{32} & M_{33} \end{pmatrix}, \quad \mathbf{v} = \begin{pmatrix} v_1 \\ v_2 \\ v_3 \end{pmatrix}.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Since $A \mathbf{v} = -4 \mathbf{v}$, we can write $A$ as $A = \pm 4 I$, where $I$ is the $3 \times 3$ identity matrix. The identity matrix has diagonal entries of $1$ on the diagonal, suggesting that the matrix $\mathbf{M}$ should be composed of $4$-step prec-validation as its superdiagonal entries equal $1$, which represents the entry in the $1 \times 1$ submatrix of $\mathbf{M}$. 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m In simpler terms, the $3 \times 3$ matrix $\mathbf{M}$ should be constructed with entries that have a $1$ at the top of the diagonal and the rest as $0$. One possible solution that satisfies this condition is:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \mathbf{M} = \begin{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 4 & 0 & 0 \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 0 & 4 & 0 \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 0 & 0 & 4
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \end{pmatrix}.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m So the final answer is:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \boxed{\begin{pmatrix} 4 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & 4 \end{pmatrix}}<|endoftext|>
