[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To find the length of a line segment given the endpoints, we can use the distance formula. The distance formula between two points \((x_1, y_1)\) and \((x_2, y_2)\) is:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m d = \sqrt{(x_2 - x_1)^2 + (y_2 - y_1)^2}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m In this problem, the endpoints are \((-3, 7)\) and \((2, -5)\). So, substituting these coordinates into the distance formula, we get:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m d = \sqrt{(2 - (-3))^2 + (-5 - 7)^2}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m First, simplify the expressions inside the parentheses:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m d = \sqrt{(2 + 3)^2 + (-5 - 7)^2}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m d = \sqrt{5^2 + (-12)^2}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Calculate the squares:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m d = \sqrt{25 + 144}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
