[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Certainly! To find all the possible integer roots of the polynomial \(3x^3 + a_2 x^2 + a_1 x - 6 = 0\), we can use the Rational Root Theorem. This theorem states that if the polynomial has a rational root, then its potential root must be a fraction \(\frac{p}{q}\), where \(p\) is a factor of the constant term (here, \(-6\)) and \(q\) is a factor of the leading coefficient (here, \(3\)).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m The factors of \(-6\) are \( \pm 1, \pm 2, \pm 3, \pm 6 \).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m The factors of \(3\) are \( \pm 1, \pm 3 \).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Therefore, the possible integer roots are all the rational numbers that can be expressed in the form \(\frac{p}{q}\), where \(p\) is a factor of \(-6\) and \(q\) is a factor of \(3\). These rational numbers are:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ \pm 1, \pm 2, \pm 3, \pm 6 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Each of these values of \(p\) divided by the corresponding value of \(q\) will give us a root of the polynomial. So the list of possible integer roots is:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \boxed{1, -1, 2, -2, 3, -3, 6, -6}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
