[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m A new train goes $20\%$ farther than an older train in the same amount of time. During the time it takes the older train to go 200 miles, how many miles can the newer train complete?
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To determine how many miles the newer train can complete, we need to recognize that the distances completed by the older and newer trains are in the same ratio, specifically 20% to 100%. This means the older train goes 100 miles (120%) of its total distance and the newer train goes 20 miles (200%) of this.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m First, we need to figure out how many miles the older train can complete in the same time. Since the older train goes 120% of its total distance, and the newer train goes 200% of this total distance, we can set up the following relationship:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \text{Distance traveled by older train} \times 1.20 = \text{Distance traveled by newer train}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Solving for the former, we get:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \text{Distance traveled by newer train} = \frac{\text{Distance traveled by older train}}{1.20}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Given that the older train goes 200 miles, we can substitute this value into the equation:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \text{Distance traveled by newer train} = \frac{200}{1.20}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Now, we perform the division:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \text{Distance traveled by newer train} = \frac{200}{1.20} = \frac{200 \times 100}{120} = \frac{20000}{120} = \frac{20000 \div 20}{120 \div 20} = \frac{1000}{6} = 166.67
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Therefore, the number of miles the newer train can complete is \(\boxed{166.67}\).<|endoftext|>
