[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To find the matrix that corresponds to rotating about the origin by an angle of \(\frac{45^\circ}{2} = 22.5^\circ\) clockwise, we need to find angles for the rotation vectors about the origin after squaring them. The rotation matrix for a counterclockwise rotation by \(\theta\) is given by:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m R(\theta) = \begin{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \cos \theta & -\sin \theta \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \sin \theta & \cos \theta
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \end{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m For a clockwise rotation by \(-\theta\), the rotation matrix is:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m R(-\theta) = \begin{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \cos (-\theta) & -\sin (-\theta) \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \sin (-\theta) & \cos (-\theta)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \cos \theta & \sin \theta \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m -\sin \theta & \cos \theta
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \end{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Adding these matrices together for \(-22.5^\circ\) (which is \(-\frac{45^\circ}{2}\)):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \begin{pmatrix}
