[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To find the number of ways April can arrange her 8 plants (4 basil plants and 4 tomato plants) with tomato plants next to each other, we can treat each tomato plant as a single unit and then consider the arrangements within this unit.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m First, let's consider the 4 tomato plants as a single unit. This creates 5 units to arrange: one tomato unit along with the 4 basil plants. Therefore, we have 6 units to arrange in a row:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 1 tomato unit * 4 basil plants = 6 units.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Since each unit can be arranged in any order (basil plant in the first position, second in the second position, third in the third position, and the fourth in the fourth position), we have 6 units to choose from. This process involves arranging 6 items.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m The number of ways to arrange these 6 items is 6!.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m So, we calculate 6! as:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 6! = 6 × 5 × 4 × 3 × 2 × 1
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Now, substitute the factorials of the units:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 6!(4 tammy, 3 basil) = 6 × 5 × 4 × 3 × 2 × 1 × 4 × 3 × 2 × 1 × 3 × 2 × 1
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m It is 720.
