[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To find the number of positive two-digit integers that have an odd number of positive factors, we need to understand the properties of the factorization of the numbers.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 1. A number has an odd number of positive factors if and only if it is a perfect square. This is because factors of a number come in pairs, and if the number is a perfect square with more than one pair of factors, that's not enough to make the total count of factors odd.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 2. The two-digit integers range from 10 to 99. 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 3. The integers that are perfect squares are 4, 9, 16, 25, 36, 49, 64, 81. These are the only two-digit integers that can have an odd number of positive factors.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 4. Now, let's count these:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    - 4 has 3 factors (1, 2, 4)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    - 9 has 3 factors (1, 3, 9)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    - 16 has 4 factors (1, 2, 4, 8)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    - 25 has 3 factors (1, 5, 25)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    - 36 has 6 factors (1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 9, 12, 18, 36)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    - 49 has 3 factors (1, 7, 49)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    - 64 has 4 factors (1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    - 81 has 4 factors (1, 3, 9, 27, 81)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m So, among the two-digit integers, 4, 9, 16, 25, 36, 49, 64, and 81, there are 8 numbers without any positive factors to be considered, since not all 8 numbers can be perfect squares.<|endoftext|>
