[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m A point $P$ is chosen at random in the interior of equilateral triangle $ABC$. What is the probability that $\triangle ABP$ has a greater area than each of $\triangle ACP$ and $\triangle BCP$?
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m The probability that \(\triangle ABP\) has a greater area than each of \(\triangle ACP\) and \(\triangle BCP\) depends on the area of \(\triangle ABC\)_. Since \(\triangle ABC\) is equilateral, each of \(\triangle ACP\), \(\triangle ABP\), and \(\triangle BCP\) has an area that is equal to \(\frac{1}{3}\) of \(\triangle ABC\) since the total area of \(ABC\) is divided into three equal parts.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m ####
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Translatef
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m We can calculate the areas of the smaller triangles with equal height from the base \(\triangle ABC\)_, as \(\frac{1}{3}\) of \(\triangle ABC\). Therefore, the area of \(\triangle ABP\) is \(1\) times greater than the area of any of the \(\triangle BCP\) or \(\triangle ACP\) (each is \( \frac{1}{3} \) of \(\triangle ABC\).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Thus, the probability of \(\triangle ABP\) having a greater area than each of \(\triangle ACP\) or \(\triangle BCP\) is \( \frac{1}{3} \) of the probability of any \(\triangle BCP\) or \(\triangle ACP\) having a greater area than that of \(\triangle ABP\).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \(\therefore \Bigg( \frac{1}{3}\Bigg)^3= \frac{1}{27}\)
