[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 1. **Identify the domain of the function:**
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    The function \( k(x) = \frac{2x + 7}{x - 3} \) is undefined when the denominator is zero. Thus, solving for the value of \( x \) that makes the denominator zero:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    x - 3 = 0 \implies x = 3
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    Therefore, the domain of the function \( k(x) \) is all real numbers except \( x = 3 \). This can be written as:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    \text{Domain: } (-\infty, 3) \cup (3, \infty)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 2. **Analyze the behavior of the function as \( x \) approaches infinity:**
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    To understand the behavior of the function as \( x \) approaches infinity, consider:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    k(x) = \frac{2x + 7}{x - 3}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    As \( x \) becomes very large (i.e., \( x \to \infty \)), the term \( 2x + 7 \) dominates over the \( x - 3 \). Thus, the function \( k(x) \) approaches:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    \lim_{x \to \infty} \frac{2x + 7}{x - 3} = \lim_{x \to \infty} \frac{2x}{x} = \lim_{x \to \infty} 2 = 2
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    Similarly, as \( x \) moves away from \( 3 \) (i.e., as \( x \to -\infty \)), the term \( 2x - 7 \) becomes negative, which makes the denominator negative. Therefore, \( k(x) \) approaches:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    \lim_{x \to -\infty} \frac{2x + 7}{x - 3} = -\infty
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 3. **Conclusion about the range:**
