[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m This problem can be solved using the fundamental counting principle, which states that if there are $a$ ways one event can occur and $b$ ways another event can occur, then there are $a \cdot b$ ways two events can occur simultaneously.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m In this problem, there are 12 doors to enter or leave the building, and we are interested in finding the number of ways to enter one door and leave two doors later. 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Therefore, there are 12 ways to enter the building, and the remaining 11 ways to leave the building.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m So, to find the total number of ways, we multiply the number of ways to enter the building (12) by the number of ways to leave the building (11):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \\boxed{132}<|endoftext|>
