[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m A circle is circumscribed about an equilateral triangle with side lengths of $9$ units each.  What is the area of the circle, in square units? Express your answer in terms of $\pi$.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To find the area of the circle, we start by determining the radius of the circumscribed circle of the equilateral triangle. An equilateral triangle has all its sides equal, so the distance between two vertices is twice the length of a side (9 units), which gives us a side length of \(3 \times 9 = 27\) units.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m In a circle, all the medians are drawn, and they intersect at the centroid. For an equilateral triangle, this centroid coincides with the incenter, which means the radius \(R\) of the circumscribed circle is half the length of a side of the equilateral triangle. Thus, \(R = \frac{27}{2} = 13.5\) units.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m The area \(A\) of a circle (or equivalently, the area of a circumcircle or inscribed circle) is given by the formula:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ A = \pi R^2 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Substituting the radius \(R = 13.5\):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ A = \pi (13.5)^2 = \pi \times 182.25 = 182.25\pi \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Thus, the area of the circle is:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ \boxed{182.25\pi} \]<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m [Ground Truth]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 27\pi
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
