[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m What is the remainder when $(x + 1)^{2010}$ is divided by $x^2 + x + 1$?
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $\text{First}$, we introduce the following substitutions:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $ \begin{cases} x= t-\frac{1}{t}\text{ or},t\neq0, \\ 1=t^2+\frac{1}{t+1}\text{ or},t+1\neq1,\end{cases} $
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m which lead to
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m (x+1)^{2010} = \left(t-\frac{1}{t}\right)^{2010}\left(t+1\right)^{2009}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m   = \left(t^2+\frac{1}{t+1}\right)^{2009}\left(t-\frac{1}{t}\right)^{1009}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m   .
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Then according to the fundamental theorem of algebra, $x^2+1$ has $2010+1/1=1001$ roots.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m It comes that we have 1001 factors:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \begin{aligned}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m x^2+1 & =-2t \\ xom}\end{aligned}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $$ 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m from which we can derive the following values for these factors:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m x, x+1, x^2+1,  x+1, \ldots, x^2+1\pm\frac{4}{1001}t \\ = \pm\binom{1001}{2}-1001-2008t \\ = \pm1001\binom{1000}{2}-1001-2008t \text{,}\\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \end{aligned}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Ignoring the constant terms, we find those factors to be
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m x+y,\ x+\sqrt{-x}, \ \sqrt{-y}, \ \sqrt{xy}, \ \sqrt{-x-y}, \ \sqrt{-x+y},
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \sqrt{-xy},
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m making it easy to see that they all satisfy the given condition.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Finally, the given condition is equivalent to one of
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m x+1=y \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m (x+1)(x+\sqrt{-x})=y^{2}-xy \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m x(y^{2}-xy+x+\sqrt{-x})=y^{2}-xy \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m x=1, \ \ y^{2-1}-x=y^{2-1}-1=x
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Letting $x-1=q$, we have
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 1=q+\sqrt{-q}, \ \ y^{2-(q+1)}-q=y^{2-q},
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m which means that $y|\sqrt{q+1}-q$ for any quadratic root pair $q$, $r$ with $r \neq 0$ and $r\neq 1$.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Hence, we get
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m q+\sqrt{-q}=(\sqrt{-q}-1)-(\sqrt{-q}+1)=\sqrt{-q}-1-\sqrt{-q}-1=- 2,
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m with $q$ being replaced by $r$.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Since $R(x-1)=x+1$, we just have to check which $q$ fixes $x+1$ with $R(y^{2-(q+1)}-q)$ being $y^{2-q} -.q^{2}$ to be $y^{2-q}-y^{2-q-1}+q-q^{2}=1$ for any rational number $q$.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \sqrt{-q}-1-q=\pm\sqrt{q^{2}-1-q^{2}}+q-1\pm\sqrt{q^{2}-1-q^{2}}=-2,\label{eq1}\\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m q=\sqrt[-2]{q^{2}-1-q^{2}}+q\pm1\pm\sqrt[-2]{q^{2}-1-q^{2}}=-2.\label{eq2}\\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m By the difference equation $\sqrt{x^{2}}=x$, the solutions are
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m x_{1}=2-x^{2/3}, \ \ x_{2}=-x^{2}+2 x^{2/3}, \ \ x_{3}=x^{2}-2 x^{2/3},\,\ldots
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Since $x=1$ and $x+1$ is not a fixed point of $x+1$, and it is not equal to zero, the only solution of the problem is
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m f(x)=1+x^{2/3}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m The answer is $\boxed{1+x^{2/3}}$. FTC explores the idea of Fibonacci numbers, and uses the logic from the beady eye (that mathematicians have full sight of the celestial).
