[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Judge if the following sets are polyhedra:  
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m (1) Cuboid which has a square base  
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m (2) Hexahedron which has a pentagonal base  
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m (3) Tetrahedron which has a triangular base  
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m (4) Quadrilateral prism which has two rectangular sides
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To determine whether each given set of geometric shapes is a polyhedron, we need to understand what a polyhedron is. A polyhedron is a solid figure with a flat surface called a face, and pentagons (and hexagons) are typically considered "cubes" when this problem is specifically talking about cuboids.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Let's analyze each set:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 1. **Cuboid**: A cuboid is a three-dimensional shape with fixed lengths for all its edges. It has a square base and rectangular sides perpendicular to the base. Definition 8-1 describes a cuboid as having "six faces, all of which are rectangles, and four of the faces are congruent." While some arguments might be made for a cuboid to have a square base, it is generally considered to have a square base because it fits its definition with other cuboids in polyhedron classification.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m So, **(1) Cuboid** is both a polyhedron and a "cuboid" in terms of its definition.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 2. **Hexahedron**: A hexahedron is a three-dimensional shape with six faces, each of which is a polygon. Hexahedra include all three-dimensional shapes with six faces, such as a cube (a special case of a hexahedron), which has square faces. Hexahedra do not have except for some special cases (tetrahedra and octahedra) being considered "cubes" (which all hexahedra are, but not here).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m So, **(2) Hexahedron** is only one specific type of polyhedron, not all polyhedrons, and it does not fit the definition of a "cuboid."
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 3. **Tetrahedron**: A tetrahedron is a polyhedron with four triangular faces, six edges, and four vertices. This definition includes all types of polyhedrons that have four triangular faces, which is more than the definition of a "cuboid" (whom we consider to have square base due to definition 8-1).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m So, **(3) Tetrahedron** is only one specific type of polyhedron, not all polyhedrons, and it does not fit the definition of a "cuboid."
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 4. **Quadrilateral prism**: A quadrilateral prism is a polyhedron with two parallel and congruent quadrilateral bases and four rectangular lateral faces. This definition includes all types of polyhedrons with at least two sides of the same shape (possibly more than one side of the same shape) and at least four faces where each pair of adjacent faces are identical at their edges.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Any quadrilateral prism can be considered a "cuboid" because this definition aligns with a cuboid definition.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m So, **(4) Quadrilateral prism** is not a "cuboid" and is another specific type of polyhedron, not all polyhedrons.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Therefore, the final answer is:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \boxed{\text{(1) \text{cuboid}, (2) \text{only one specific type of polyhedron}, (3) only one specific type of polyhedron, (4) not all polyhedrons}}
