[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Compute $\sin 6^\circ \sin 42^\circ \sin 66^\circ \sin 78^\circ.$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m We are given the sum of sines: $\sin 6^\circ \sin 42^\circ \sin 66^\circ \sin 78^\circ$.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To solve this, we intend to use the product-to-sum identities to simplify the expression.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Starting from $\sin x = 2 \sin \frac{x}{2} \cos \frac{x}{2}$, we get:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $\sin 66^\circ = 2 \sin \frac{66^\circ}{2} \cos \frac{66^\circ}{2} = 2 \sin 33^\circ \cos 33^\circ$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $\sin 78^\circ = 2 \sin \frac{78^\circ}{2} \cos \frac{78^\circ}{2} = 2 \sin 39^\circ \cos 39^\circ$, where $\cos 33^\circ = \cos 36^\circ$, $\cos 36^\circ = \cos (60^\circ - 24^\circ) = \sin 24^\circ$.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m But $\sin 33^\circ + \sin 39^\circ \ne 1$. Hence we use the complementary identity: $\sin(x \pm y) = \sin x \cos y \pm \cos x \sin y$.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Then, $2 \sin 33^\circ \cos 33^\circ = \sin 66^\circ = \sin (60^\circ + 6^\circ) = \sin 60^\circ \cos 6^\circ + \cos 60^\circ \sin 6^\circ = \frac{1}{2} \cos 6^\circ + \frac{1}{2} \sin 6^\circ = \cdot \cos 6^\circ$$\pm\cdot\sin 6^\circ$, treating each positive and negative case as the argument of our trigonometric function. We can treat the positive case as $\sin x = \cos (\90^\circ - x)$, then $\pm\cos 6^\circ$ form the positive case and $\pm\sin 6^\circ$ form the opposite case.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m When the expression is positive, $2 \sin 33^\circ \cos 33^\circ = 2 \cdot \cos 6^\circ \cdot \sin 6^\circ = \cdot 1$ by the product-to-sum identities.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m When the expression is negative, $2 \sin 33^\circ \cos 33^\circ = − 1$.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m So, the sum of all possible sines on the right side is $\pm \cos 6^\circ + \pm \sin 6^\circ$, the result finding is the sum of our negative and positive sides, i.e. $\pm 1$.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Putting everything together, the given mathematical statement becomes $\pm 1 \cos 6^\circ \pm \pm 1 \sin 6^\circ = \pm 1$. Hence, the final result is $\boxed{{\pm 1}}$.<|endoftext|>
