[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Human: Let $P$ be the plane passing through the origin with normal vector $\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\ -2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}$. Find the matrix $\mathbf{P}$ that puts the points $(1,2,3),$ $(3,4,5),$ $(2,0,3),$ and $(O,0,6),$ in 共空间 共次1折线 position. (Note: The moves are to rotate the plane so that $(1,2,3),(3,4,5)$ $180^{\circ}$ around $\mathbf{P}$ instead of $(1,2,3),(3,4,5),(2,0,3),(0,6)$ O,Q, and P. The standard rotation transformations should be used.)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Assistant: To find the matrix \(\mathbf{P}\) that puts the points \((1,2,3)\), \((3,4,5)\), \((2,0,3)\), and \((O,0,6)\) in homogeneous position, we need to determine how the plane passes through each of these points. The plane passes through the origin and has a normal vector \(\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\ -2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}\). It is perpendicular to both the x-axis and y-axis. Therefore, it could be a plane with normal vector, say \(\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\ -2 \\ -1 \end{pmatrix}\), passing through all four points.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m The matrix \(\mathbf{P}\) must be a linear transformation that rotates the plane so that \(\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}\) becomes \(\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\ -2 \\ -1 \end{pmatrix}\). This means we need a 3x3 matrix that transforms the standard basis vectors of \(\mathbb{R}^3\) under \(\mathbf{P}\).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m The general form of a rotation matrix in \(\mathbb{R}^3\) around the axis \([a b c]_{\hat{b}} = (0 0 0 1)_\hat{b}\) by an angle \(\theta\) is:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \mathbf{R}(a) = \begin{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \cos \theta & -\sin \theta & a \\ -\sin \theta & \mathbf{c} a^2 + c^2 & a^2 - c^2 \\ a & \mathbf{c}^2 a^2 + c^2 b^2 + 2 b^2c & c^2 a^2 - 2 b^2 c + \mathbf{c}^2 b^2 \end{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m where \(\mathbf{c} = \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ -1 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}\).
