[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To determine the least number of digits in a repeating block of \( \frac{8}{11} \) when expressed as a decimal, we need to find the decimal representation of \( \frac{8}{11} \).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m First, let's find a suitable \( x \) that when multiplied by 11, gives 8:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 11x = 8 - x
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 12x = 8
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m x = \frac{8}{12} = \frac{2}{3}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Now, \( x = \frac{2}{3} \). We need to find the decimal expansion of \( \frac{2}{3} \).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To do this, we can perform long division of 2 by 3:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \begin{array}{r|l}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 3 & 2.000000 \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \hline
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 0 & 0 \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \end{array} \quad \text{(1st term)}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m We bring down the 0 to get the new number 20, and divide into 20:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 1 \quad 4.9999991 \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \hline
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 20 & 0 \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 1 & 4.9999991 \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 10 & 0 \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 1 & 4.9999991 \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 47 & 0 \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 1 & 4.9999991 \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 48 & 0 \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 1 & 4.9999991 \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 47 & 0 \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \end{array}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m We continue this process until we get a remainder of 0. The repeating block of digits will be 289147289.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Thus, the decimal expansion of \( \frac{8}{11} \) is \( 0.\overline{289147289} \).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m The least number of digits in a repeating block of \( \frac{8}{11} \) is \(\boxed{9}\).<|endoftext|>
