[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To convert a point from rectangular coordinates to polar coordinates, we use the following formulas:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 1. \( r = \sqrt{x^2 + y^2} \)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 2. \( \theta = \arctan\left(\frac{y}{x}\right) \)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 3. \( \phi = \pi - \theta \) (for angles between \(0^{\circ}\) and \(360^{\circ}\))
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Given the point \((-2, -2)\):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 1. Calculate \( r \):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    r = \sqrt{(-2)^2 + (-2)^2} = \sqrt{4 + 4} = \sqrt{8} = 2\sqrt{2}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 2. Calculate \( \theta \):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    \theta = \arctan\left(\frac{-2}{-2}\right) = \arctan(1) = \frac{\pi}{4}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
