[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To find the matrix corresponding to a dilation centered at the origin with a scale factor of \(-3\), we need to understand how dilation affects vectors in \(\mathbb{R}^2\). Specifically, a dilation with a scale factor \(k\) shifts every vector \((x, y)\) by \((kx, ky)\), where \(\mathbf{v} = (x, y)\).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Given:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m - The scale factor \(k = -3\).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m The rule for dilation can be written in matrix form as follows:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ \mathbf{v}' = k \mathbf{v} = (-3) \mathbf{v} \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Where \(\mathbf{v}'\) is the scaled vector by \(k\).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Given \(\mathbf{v} = (x, y)\), the dilation can be represented by the matrix:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ \begin{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m k & 0 \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 0 & k
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \end{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Substituting \(k = -3\) into the matrix:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ \begin{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m -3 & 0 \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 0 & -3
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \end{pmatrix} \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m So, the matrix that corresponds to a dilation centered at the origin with a scale factor of \(-3\) is:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ \boxed{\begin{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m -3 & 0 \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 0 & -3
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \end{pmatrix}} \]<|endoftext|>
