[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To find the vector \(\mathbf{v}\) such that \(\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{v} = \begin{pmatrix} 90 \\ 30 \\ 30 \end{pmatrix}\), we need to understand that the cross product of any vector with itself is always the zero vector. This is because the cross product of a vector with itself is a vector that is perpendicular to the original vector and has a magnitude equal to the square of the original vector's magnitude. Therefore, the cross product of any vector with itself is \(\mathbf{0}\).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Given this, we can set up the equation:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{v} = \begin{pmatrix} 90 \\ 30 \\ 30 \end{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Since \(\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{v} = \mathbf{0}\), the equation simplifies to:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
