[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Let's start by analyzing the given information in the problem. We know that $TS \parallel RQ$, so we can use the property of parallel lines and corresponding angles.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m First, we'll find $\angle TPQ$ using the fact that the sum of the angles on a straight line plus $\angle PRQ$ equals $180^{\circ}$, and since $TS \parallel RQ$, we know that $\angle TPQ$ is the same as $\angle PRQ$.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m So, we have $\angle TPQ = \angle PRQ$. Let's call the measure of $\angle TPQ$ or $\angle PRQ$ as $x$.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m We are given the following angles:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m - $\angle RPQ = 65^{\circ}$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m - $\angle TSQ = 145^{\circ}$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m - We need to find $\angle TPQ$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Since $TS \parallel RQ$, we have $\angle TPQ$ as part of $\angle TSQ$, so:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \angle TPQ = \angle TSQ = 145^{\circ}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Now, we can use the fact that the sum of the angles in a triangle is $180^{\circ}$:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \angle RPQ + \angle RSQ + \angle PRQ = 180^{\circ}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Since $\angle NSQ$ is part of $\angle TNQ$, we can use the fact that $\angle TPQ = \angle NSQ$:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \angle TPQ + \angle RNQ + \angle PRQ = 180^{\circ}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Substituting the known angles:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 145^{\circ} + \angle NQR + \angle PRQ = 180^{\circ}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Since $\angle NQR$ is part of $\angle NRP$, and knowing that $\angle PRQ$ is $145^{\circ}$:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \angle PRQ + \angle NQR + \angle(PRQP) = 180^{\circ}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To find $\angle PRQP$, we note that $\angle PRQP = \angle PRQ$ because they are on the same side of the transversal $TS$:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \angle PRQP = \angle PRQ
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m So, $\angle PRQP = 145^{\circ}$.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Finally, substituting $\angle PRQP$ back into our equation:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 145^{\circ} + \angle NQR + 145^{\circ} = 180^{\circ}
