[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To evaluate the sum \(\log_{10}(\tan 1^{\circ})+\log_{10}(\tan 2^{\circ})+\log_{10}(\tan 3^{\circ})+\cdots+\log_{10}(\tan 88^{\circ})+\log_{10}(\tan 89^{\circ})\), we will use properties of logarithms and trigonometric identities.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m ### Step 1: Simplify each term using properties of logarithms
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m The sum of the logarithms is:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \log_{10}(\tan 1^{\circ})+\log_{10}(\tan 2^{\circ})+\log_{10}(\tan 3^{\circ})+\cdots+\log_{10}(\tan 88^{\circ})+\log_{10}(\tan 89^{\circ})
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Using the property \(\log_a x + \log_a y = \log_a (xy)\), we can combine the terms:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \log_{10}(\tan 1^{\circ} \cdot \tan 2^{\circ} \cdot \tan 3^{\circ} \cdots \tan 88^{\circ} \cdot \tan 89^{\circ})
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m ### Step 2: Use a known trigonometric identity
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m We will use the identity for the product of tangents of angles near 90 degrees. Specifically, for angles \(n\) and \(m\):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \tan(n\pi - m) = \tan 90^{\circ}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Thus:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \tan(n\pi - m) = \tan(n\pi - (n - m)) = \tan(90^{\circ} - (n - m)) = \cot(n - m) = \frac{1}{\tan(n - m)}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m ### Step 3: Calculate one term at a time
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m We will consider a cyclic sum:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \tan 1^{\circ} \cdot \tan 2^{\circ} \cdot \tan 3^{\circ} \cdots \tan 88^{\circ} \cdot \tan 89^{\circ}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Simplify each pair \((\tan n - m)\):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \tan 90^{\circ} - \tan 1^{\circ} = 0
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \tan 90^{\circ} - \tan 2^{\circ} = 0
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \tan 90^{\circ} - \tan 3^{\circ} = 0
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \tan 90^{\circ} - \tan 89^{\circ} = 0
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Since \(\tan 90^{\circ}\) is undefined, each pair \((\tan n - m)\) is zero:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \tan 90^{\circ} - \tan m = 0 \quad \text{for all} \quad n \neq m
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m ### Step 4: Count the number of pairs
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m There are 88 pairs, each contributing a zero:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \text{Total sum} = 88 \times 0 = 0
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Thus, the value of the sum is:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \boxed{0}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
