[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m A two-inch cube ($2\times2\times2$) of silver weighs 3 pounds and is worth $\$200$. How much is a three-inch cube of silver worth?  Round your answer to the nearest dollar.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To determine the value of a three-inch cube of silver, we start by finding the density of the silver. The volume of a cube is given by the formula:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[V = s^3\]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m where \(s\) is the side length of the cube. For a three-inch cube, the side length \(s\) is 3 inches. Therefore, the volume of the cube is:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[V = 3^3 = 27 \text{ cubic inches}\]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Next, we know that 2 pounds of silver weigh 200 dollars. We need to find the weight of 1 pound of silver. Using the given values, the weight of 1 pound of silver is:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[200 \text{ dollars} = \frac{2 \text{ pounds}}{1 \text{ pound/unit}} \times 27 \text{ cubic inches}\]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m So, the weight of 1 pound of silver is:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[200 \text{ dollars} \times 27 \text{ cubic inches/lb} = 5400 \text{ cubic inches/pound}\]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Now, we find the weight of a one-inch cube. The volume of a one-inch cube is:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[1^3 = 1 \text{ cubic inch}\]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m The weight of 1 cubic inch of silver is:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[5400 \text{ cubic inches} \times 2 \text{ pounds} = 10800 \text{ pounds}\]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To find the weight of a three-inch cube, we use the fraction of the weight of a one-inch cube to the volume of the cube:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[\text{Weight of a three-inch cube} = \frac{1}{1^3} \times 5400 = 5400 \text{ pounds}\]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Rounding the answer to the nearest dollar, we get:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[\boxed{5400}\]<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m [Ground Truth]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \$675
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
