[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To find the midpoint of a line segment given its endpoints, we can use the midpoint formula. The midpoint \(M\) of a line segment with endpoints \((x_1, y_1)\) and \((x_2, y_2)\) is calculated using the following formula:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ M = \left( \frac{x_1 + x_2}{2}, \frac{y_1 + y_2}{2} \right) \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Given the endpoints \((-3, 4)\) and \((7, -6)\), we can substitute these coordinates into the formula.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ M = \left( \frac{-3 + 7}{2}, \frac{4 + (-6)}{2} \right) \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Simplify the expressions:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ M = \left( \frac{4}{2}, \frac{-2}{2} \right) \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ M = (2, -1) \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Therefore, the midpoint of the segment is \(\boxed{(2, -1)}\).<|endoftext|>
