[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m If incircle of a triangle is tangent to two sides of the triangle and externally tangent to the opposite side, how to mean the radius of smaller circle I have two sides of a triangle and the ratio of the circumference to the other side. The smaller circle drawn inside the triangle and tangent to the other two sides of the triangle (the smaller circle is drawn inside the incircle of the triangle and tangent to one side) has a certain circumference. How can I find the radius of the circle?
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m For a circle centred on the central angle swept by the smaller circle, which is subtended by the radius of the circle:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $$\pi=\frac{\text{length of arc swept by the smaller circle}}{\text{length of the circumference of the circle}}$$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m From there you can solve for $\pi$.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m $$\frac{\operatorname{circumference of the smaller circle}}{\text{circumference of the circle}}=\frac{2A}{2\theta}=2\times\frac{A}{R\text{ (small circle)}}$$
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m So $R=AR/\pi$.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m As the radius is tangent to the inscribed circle and also tangent to the third side, the distance between the inscribed circle and the tangent segment to the larger circle is $r$ and that distance from the center of the larger circle to any of the tangent points is $r$ as the third length is $\frac{AC}{2}$ and each shaded area has an area of $\frac{1}{2}\times r\times \frac{AC}{2}$.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Therefore by combining this information, a certain area can be explained by how the area of the circle and the area of the inscribed circle are combined with the area of  the shaded portions.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Can I obtain the expected value of a centered random variable? 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Let $X$ be a centered random variable.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m I know that the expectation of a centered random variable is $E(X)=0$, but what does this mean in other words? Maybe the expectation of a centered random variable is always 0, so that one does not needs to calculate the expectation?
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m It does not mean that the expected value of a centered random variable is always 0.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Let's formulate in more detail what an expected value is. 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Let us assume that the population $\boldsymbol{X}$ consists of $\boldsymbol{n}$ observations and there is a statistical interpretation of the random variable $X$ in the form of the average of these $\boldsymbol{n}$ observations. 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Let's fix a statistic $X$ that defines this average, namely $X(\boldsymbol{x}) = \mathbb{E(X)}$. Then the expected value of $X$ is
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \eqalign{
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m   & \operatorname{E}(X) = \mathbb{E}\left( {{X^{n+ 1}} - {\boldsymbol{x}_n}} \right) = \mathbb{E}\left( {0} \right) = 0.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m }
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m  
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Note²: 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m The condition $\mathbb{E}\left( {(X i _ {\boldsymbol{x}]})} \right) = \mathbb{E}\left( {{X^{\boldsymbol{x_n+1}}}} \right) = 0$ for all real $\boldsymbol{x}$ implies that 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m possible values are all monotonically decreasing (leibniz's theorem's application) and are all less than zero. 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Sometimes $X$ and $i _ {\boldsymbol{x}]}$ are called generalized moments. 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Proposition 1 (Central moment): $\mathbb{E}\left( {(X i _ {\boldsymbol{x}]})} \right) = \mathbb{E}\left( {{X^{\boldsymbol{x_n+1}}}} \right) = 0$ implies that
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \eqalign{
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m   & \mathbb{E}\left( {(i _ {\boldsymbol{x}_{\boldsymbol{i_]}})} \right) = 0;\\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m   &
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m   \mathbb{E}\left( {(i _ {\boldsymbol{x}_{\boldsymbol{i_1}}}}^{n + 1})} \right) = 0 \text{ for all }n \text{ and }{\boldsymbol{i_1}} \text{ such that
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m   }i _ {\boldsymbol{i_1}} \leq {\boldsymbol{i_2}} \text{ and }i _ {\boldsymbol{i_1}} \neq i _ {\boldsymbol{i_2}}. 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m }
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m  
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Example: The standard normal distribution is defined by $\mathbb{E}(Z) = 0$. Its central moment is
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \eqalign{
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m   & \operatorname{E}\left( {(Z^{\boldsymbol{m]}_\boldsymbol{z}]})} \approx 1 \mathbb{E}\left( {(i{}^\boldsymbol{m}{}_\boldsymbol{z}_{\boldsymbol{i_1}})} \right) \approx 0;\\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m   & \operatorname{E}\left( {(Z^{\boldsymbol{m]}_\boldsymbol{z}]})} \approx 1.  {}}\Bigot \mathds{E}\left( {\left( {\int_i^i} Z^{m}_i\,dx} \right)^{m + 1}} \right) \approx 1.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m }
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m A necessary, but not sufficient condition $:) that permits the defined "$Y$" to have $\mathbb{E}(Y) = 0$ in probability. 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m We consider the generalized moment
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \eqalign{
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m   & \mathbb{E}\left( {\left( {\int_\boldsymbol{i_1}^\boldsymbol{i_2}} Z_i^{m_i} dx} \right)^{i_2}} - Z_i^{m_{i_1}^{ }}\right) \approx 0 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m }
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m with $m_{i_1} = m-i_1$ and $i_1 \neq i_2$. In the limit $i_1 - i_2 \to 0$, the deviation from 0 must go to zero:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \eqalign{
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m   & \mathbb{E}\left( {\left( {\int_{i_1}^\infty} Z^m_i dx} \right)^{i_2}} - \int_{i_1}^\infty Z_{m_{i_1}^}dx I_{i_2} \right) \approx 0.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \\}\Bigot \mathds{I_{i_2}}\left. \to 0} \text{ as} \text{ }{i_1 - i_2 \to 0}<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
