[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 1. **Identify the range of cosine within the given interval:**
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    The cosine function, \( \cos x \), ranges from -1 to 1 for any \( x \) in the interval \( -90^\circ < x < 90^\circ \).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 2. **Determine the range of the square root function:**
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    The square root function, \( \sqrt{\cos x} \), will produce a range where \( \sqrt{\cos x} \) takes values between \(0\) and \(1\) because \( \cos x \) ranges from approximately \(-0.2928\) to \(0.2928\) (approximately \(24.9^\circ\) to \(45.1^\circ\)).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 3. **Determine the range of the logarithmic function:**
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    The base of the logarithm is 2, a positive number. The logarithm function, \( \log_2 \), alone will also range from \(-\infty\) (for very small values of \(y\)) to \(\infty\) (for very large values of \(y\)). To check if \( y = \log_2 (\sqrt{\cos x}) \) can take on any value in between, we need to consider the transformation \( y = \log_2 (\sqrt{\cos x}) \). The logarithm function \( \log_x \) can take on any positive real value as \( x \) ranges from 0 to \(\infty\) without limitation, because \( \sqrt{\cos x} \) ranges between 0 and 1.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m However, we need to find the specific value of \( y \). Since \( y = \log_2 (\sqrt{\cos x}) \), the range of \( y \) will be determined by \( \cos x \). The minimum value of \( y = \log_2 (\sqrt{\cos x}) \) occurs when \( \cos x = 0 \), which is at \( y = \log_2 (0) \). This is undefined, but considering the nature of logarithms and the argument, we can say the range of \( y \) is from \(-\infty\) to \(\log_2 (1) = 0\) within the context of the interval.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 4. **Conclusion:**
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    Therefore, the range of the function \( y = \log_2 (\sqrt{\cos x}) \) for \( -90^\circ < x < 90^\circ \) is \( y = (-\infty, 0] \).
