[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m As $n$ ranges over the positive integers, what is the maximum possible value for the greatest common divisor of $11n+3$ and $6n+1$?
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To find the maximum possible value for the greatest common divisor (GCD) of \(11n + 3\) and \(6n + 1\), we will use the properties of GCD. Let \(d\) be the GCD of \(11n + 3\) and \(6n + 1\). Then \(d\) must divide any linear combination of \(11n + 3\) and \(6n + 1\). In particular, we will look at the combinations involving \(n\):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 1. \(d\) divides \(6n + 1\)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 2. \(d\) divides \(11n + 3\)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Using the Euclidean algorithm, we can express \(d\) as a linear combination of \(11n + 3\) and \(6n + 1\). We start with the linear combination equation:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[d = 6n + 1\]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Next, consider the pair \((11, 3)\):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[11 \cdot d = 22d\]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 22 \cdot (6n + 1) = 22 \cdot 11n + 22 \cdot 1
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[22d \equiv 22 \cdot 6n + 22 \cdot 1 \pmod{d}\]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[d \cdot (6n + 1) \equiv 22 \cdot 6n + 22 \cdot 1 \pmod{d}\]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Simplify \(22 \cdot 6n\):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[6n \cdot 22 + 22 \cdot 1 = 26n + 22\]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m So we have:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[d \cdot (22n + 22) \equiv 22 \cdot 6n + 22 \cdot 1 \pmod{d}\]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[22(n + 1) \equiv 22 \cdot 6n + 22 \cdot 1 \pmod{d}\]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[2(n + 1) \equiv 22 \pmod{d}\]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[n + 1 \equiv 11 \pmod{d}\]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[n \equiv 10 \pmod{d}\]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Thus, we need \(d\) to divide \(n + 10\). Next, consider the pair \((6, 1)\):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[6d = 6\]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[d \cdot 2 = 2 \cdot 6n + 2d \cdot 1\]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[d = 2 (2n + d) + 2d \cdot 1\]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[d = 2(2n + 1) + 2d\]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[d = 4n + 2 + 2d\]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[d = 4n + 2 + 2d\]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[d = 4n + 2 + 2d\]
