[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ 5n = 5(3k + 2) = 15k + 10. \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Next, we need to find the remainder when \(15k + 10\) is divided by 3. We can reframe this as:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ 15k + 10 \equiv k \mod 3. \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m This means that the remainder when \(5n\) is divided by 3 is the same as the remainder when \(k\) is divided by 3. Since \(k\) can be any integer, we need to determine the remainder when \(k\) is divided by 3. Let's analyze each case:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 1. If \(k \equiv 0 \mod 3\), then \(k \equiv 0 \mod 3\), so \(k = 3j\) for some integer \(j\). Therefore, the remainder when \(k\) is divided by 3 is 0.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 2. If \(k \equiv 1 \mod 3\), then \(k \equiv 1 \mod 3\), so \(k = 3j + 1\) for some integer \(j\). Therefore, the remainder when \(k\) is divided by 3 is 1.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 3. If \(k \equiv 2 \mod 3\), then \(k \equiv 2 \mod 3\), so \(k = 3j + 2\) for some integer \(j\). Therefore, the remainder when \(k\) is divided by 3 is 2.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Since \(k\) can be any integer, the remainder when \(5n\) is divided by 3 will be the same as the remainder when \(k\) is divided by 3. Therefore, the remainder when \(5n\) is divided by 3 is:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \boxed{0}
