[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m A 40-foot by 10-foot rectangular garden is enclosed by a fence. To make the garden larger, while using the same amount of fencing, its shape is changed to a square. How many square feet larger than the old garden is the new garden?
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To solve this problem, we need to calculate the area of the old rectangular garden first, and then calculate the area of the new square garden using the same amount of fencing.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m ### Step 1: Calculate the area of the old rectangular garden
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m The old rectangular garden has dimensions 40 ft by 10 ft.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ \text{Area}_{\text{old}} = \text{length} \times \text{width} = 40 \, \text{ft} \times 10 \, \text{ft} = 400 \, \text{ft}^2 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m ### Step 2: Determine the amount of fencing used for both gardens
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m The problem states that all the fencing is used for the new rectangular garden.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m ### Step 3: Calculate the perimeter of the new rectangular garden
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m The perimeter of a rectangle is given by:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ \text{Perimeter}_{\text{new}} = 2 \times (\text{length} + \text{width}) = 2 \times (40 + 10) = 2 \times 50 = 100 \, \text{ft} \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m ### Step 4: Determine the area of the new square garden
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Since all the fencing is used to form the square, the perimeter of the square is also 100 ft. Therefore, the side length of the square is:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ \text{side length}_{\text{new}} = \text{Perimeter}_{\text{square}} / 4 = 100 / 4 = 25 \, \text{ft} \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m ### Step 5: Calculate the area of the new square garden
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ \text{Area}_{\text{new}} = (\text{side length}_{\text{new}})^2 = (25 \, \text{ft})^2 = 625 \, \text{ft}^2 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m ### Step 6: Find the difference in area between the new garden and the old garden
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ \text{Difference} = \text{Area}_{\text{new}} - \text{Area}_{\text{old}} = 625 \, \text{ft}^2 - 400 \, \text{ft}^2 = 225 \, \text{ft}^2 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Hence, the new garden is \(\boxed{225}\) square feet larger than the old garden.<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m [Ground Truth]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 225
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m [Is Boxed Matched]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m True
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
