[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To determine the fractional part of the wall that Heidi can paint in 9 minutes, we need to understand her painting rate. Since she can paint a full wall in 45 minutes, her rate of painting is \(\frac{1}{45}\) of the wall per minute.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m First, we calculate how many full walls her paint can cover in 9 minutes. This can be done by dividing 9 by 45:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \frac{9}{45} = \frac{1}{5}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m This means that in 9 minutes, Heidi can paint \(\frac{1}{5}\) of the wall. To express this as a fraction of the wall, we can write:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \frac{1}{5} \times 1 = \frac{1}{5}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Therefore, the fractional part of the wall that Heidi can paint in 9 minutes is \(\boxed{\frac{1}{5}}\).<|endoftext|>
