[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To determine the number of different ways Alyssa can form a set of marbles with at least one marble, we need to consider the possible groups she can form. Alyssa has the four marbles: a red (R), a blue (B), a green (G), and a yellow (Y).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m First, let's list all the possible sets without any restrictions:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 1. {R}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 2. {B}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 3. {G}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 4. {Y}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m These are the only sets, because if we add or remove any marble, it will disrupt the condition of having at least one marble.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Next, let's consider only the sets that include at least one marble:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 1. {R}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 2. {B}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 3. {G}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 4. {Y} (still considered)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m So, out of the four possible sets, only the sets {R}, {B}, {G}, and {Y} are sets with at least one marble. There are 4 such sets.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Therefore, the number of different ways Alyssa can form a set consisting of some of the marbles, with at least one marble, is \(\boxed{4}\).<|endoftext|>
