[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Given that \(A\) and \(B\) are planes in space, we know that the intersection of \(A\) and \(B\) is the line \(A_1B_1\). This intersection line is always parallel to the line that is parallel to both planes \(A\) and \(B\). Therefore, the plane that contains this line \(A_1B_1\) is exactly the plane parallel to both planes \(A\) and \(B\).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m The line \(A_1B_1\) can be represented by the equation \(L\) where \(L = \text{line } y=0\), \(x=a_1\), \(z=b_1\). Since \(A\) and \(B\) are parallel, any plane that contains the line \(A_1B_1\) will have the same cross-section beam perpendicular to \(A_1B_1\). This cross-section beam will have a particular slope, specifically \(-1\) since the plane is at the intersection of \(A\) and \(B\). For any point \(P\) on the line, the product of the direction ratios of the normal to a plane and the direction vector of the line \(A_1B_1\) will maintain the same product when scaled by some constant, implying that the point \(P\) can lie on a parallel plane to \(A_1B_1\).
