[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To compute \(\sin 45^\circ\), we can use the fact that \(45^\circ = 45^{\circ} = 135^\circ - 180^\circ\) because the sine function has a phase shift of \(180^\circ\). More specifically, \(\sin(135^\circ - 180^\circ) = \sin(-45^\circ)\). The sine function is odd, meaning \(\sin(-45^\circ) = -\sin(45^\circ)\).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m We know that \(\sin(45^\circ) = \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\). Therefore,
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \sin(-45^\circ) = -\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m So, \(\sin 45^\circ = -\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Putting the final answer in a box, we have:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \boxed{-\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
