[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    - \( 121 \div 3 \approx 40.33 \) (not divisible by 3)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    - \( 144 \div 3 = 48 \) (not divisible by 3)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    - \( 169 \div 3 \approx 56.33 \) (not divisible by 3)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    - \( 196 \div 3 \approx 65.33 \) (not divisible by 3)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    - \( 225 \div 3 = 75 \) (divisibly by 3)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    - \( 256 \div 3 \approx 85.33 \) (not divisible by 3)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    - \( 289 \div 3 \approx 96.33 \) (not divisible by 3)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    - \( 324 \div 3 = 108 \) (divisibly by 3)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    - \( 361 \div 3 \approx 120.33 \) (not divisible by 3)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    - \( 400 \div 3 \approx 133.33 \) (not divisible by 3)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    From the list, the two-digit perfect squares that are divisible by 3 are \( 196 \) and \( 289 \).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 3. **Count the number of such perfect squares:**
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    There are 2 two-digit perfect squares that are divisible by 3.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Therefore, the final answer is:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
