[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To find the smallest base-10 integer that can be represented as \(AA_5\) and \(BB_7\), we need to express these numbers in a common base. Conventionally, in base 5 and base 7, numbers are represented using digits up to 4 for base 5 (since \(5^1 = 5\) and \(5^2 = 25\) which is also likely to be much smaller than any digit in base 7 before 6) or digits up to 6 for base 7. However, the least common multiple (LCM) of 5 and 7 will give us the smallest and most suitable base within which these representations can be expressed.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m The LCM of 5 and 7 is \(5 \times 7 = 35\). This means that any integer that can be expressed as a 3-digit number in base 5 (i.e., an integer from \(100_5\) to \(344_5\)) will also have the same form as an integer from \(100_7\) to \(344_7\).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Let's convert each base from 35 into a base 10 number step by step for both sets of numbers.
