[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m For what value of $n$ is the five-digit number $\underline{7n933}$ divisible by 33? (Note: the underlining is meant to indicate that the number should be interpreted as a five-digit number whose ten thousands digit is 7, whose thousands digit is $n$, and so on).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To determine the value of \( n \) for which the five-digit number \( 7n933 \) is divisible by 33, we will use the rule for divisibility by 33. A number is divisible by 33 if it is divisible by both 3 and 11.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m ### Step 1: Divisibility by 3
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m A number is divisible by 3 if the sum of its digits is divisible by 3. For the number \( 7n933 \), the sum of the digits is:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 7 + n + 9 + 3 + 3 = 22 + n
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m We need \( 22 + n \) to be divisible by 3. Let's check the possible values of \( n \):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \begin{aligned}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m &22 + 0 = 22 \quad (\text{not divisible by 3}) \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m &22 + 1 = 23 \quad (\text{not divisible by 3}) \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m &22 + 2 = 24 \quad (\text{divisible by 3}) \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m &22 + 3 = 25 \quad (\text{not divisible by 3}) \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m &22 + 4 = 26 \quad (\text{not divisible by 3}) \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m &22 + 5 = 27 \quad (\text{divisible by 3}) \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m &\text{So, } n = 5.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \end{aligned}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Thus, \( 7n933 \) is divisible by 3 when \( n = 5 \).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m ### Step 2: Divisibility by 11
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m A number is divisible by 11 if the alternating sum of its digits is divisible by 11. For the number \( 7n933 \), the alternating sum of the digits is:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 7 - n + 9 - 3 + 3 = 16 - n
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m We need \( 16 - n \) to be divisible by 11. Let's check the possible values of \( n \):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \begin{aligned}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m &16 + 1 = 17 \quad (\text{not divisible by 11}) \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m &16 + 2 = 18 \quad (\text{not divisible by 11}) \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m &16 + 3 = 19 \quad (\text{not divisible by 11}) \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m &16 + 4 = 20 \quad (\text{divisible by 11}) \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m &\text{So, } 16 = 11k + 4 \quad \text{(not valid since } k \text{ is an integer)}.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \end{aligned}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Thus, \( n = 4 \) is not the correct value.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m ### Step 3: Using the Alternating Sum Test to Verify
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To ensure that \( n = 4 \) is indeed the correct value, we check the remaining possibilities:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \begin{aligned}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m &22 + 4 = 26 \quad (\text{divisible by 3}) \\
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m &\text{So, } 7n933 \text{ is divisible by } 33
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \end{aligned}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Thus, the value of \( n \) is:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \boxed{4}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
