[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m If 52 cards are dealt to 8 people as evenly as possible, how many people will end up with fewer than 7 cards?
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To determine how many people will end up with fewer than 7 cards when 52 cards are dealt to 8 people as evenly as possible, we can follow these steps:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 1. **Calculate the average number of cards per person:**
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    \text{Average number of cards per person} = \frac{\text{Number of cards}}{\text{Number of people}} = \frac{52}{8} = 6.5
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    Since each person should receive an even number of cards, we need to adjust this to an even number. The closest even number is 6.5 rounds up to 7.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 2. **Determine how many sets of 7 cards can be formed:**
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    \text{Number of sets of 7 cards} = \frac{\text{Number of cards}}{\text{Average number of cards per person}} = \frac{52}{7} = 7.4286
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    Since 7.4286 rounded down to the nearest whole number is 7, we can have 7 sets of 7 cards each.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 3. **Find out how many cards are left over:**
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    \text{Number of leftover cards} = \text{Total cards} - (\text{Total cards per person} \times \text{Number of people}) = 52 - (6 \times 8) = 52 - 48 = 4
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 4. **Determine the number of people who receive more than 7 cards:**
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    Since each set of 7 cards is used by 1 person, and we have 4 leftover cards, the 4 leftover cards will be distributed among 4 people. To find out how many of these people will receive more than 7 cards, we can check if the leftover cards can be distributed without violating the rule of distributing cards evenly.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    If we try to distribute the leftover 4 cards with 5 people, we get:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    5 \times (7 + 4 - 7) = 5 \times \text{leftover cards} = 5 \times 4 = 20
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m    Each of the 5 people will get 4 cards, which is an odd number. Therefore, it is not feasible to distribute the leftover cards evenly among 5 people while ensuring that each person receives at least 4 cards, but it ensures that at least one person receives 7 cards (since 7 - 4 = 3 cards are left over after using 5 people).
