[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m We can start by factoring the first three terms; this gives us \[x^3 - 9x^2 + 27x - 35 = x(x^2 - 9x + 27) - 35\] \[= x(x^2 - 3x + 9) - 35\]. Then we notice that $x^3 - 9x^2 + 27x - 35 = (x-1)^3 - 35$ - that is because $(x-1)^3 = x^3 - 3x^2 + 3x - 1$, so \[x(x^2 - 3x + 9) - 35 = (x^3 - 3x^2 + 3x - 1) - 35 = (x-1)^3\] - from the fact that $x^3 - 9x^2 + 27x - 35$ is identical to $(x-1) - 1 + 9 - 35$. \[= \boxed{(x+1)}\]<|endoftext|>
