[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m The set of points with spherical coordinates of the form
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[(\rho, \theta, \phi) = \left( 1, \theta, \frac{\pi}{6} \right)\]forms a circle.  Find the radius of this circle.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To find the radius of the circle formed by the spherical coordinates \(\left( \rho, \theta, \phi \right) = \left( 1, \theta, \frac{\pi}{6} \right)\), we need to convert the spherical coordinates to Cartesian coordinates (x, y, z).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m The general formulas for converting spherical coordinates to Cartesian coordinates are:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m x = \rho \sin \phi \cos \theta
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m y = \rho \sin \phi \sin \theta
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m z = \rho \cos \phi
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Given the spherical coordinates \(\left( \rho, \theta, \phi \right) = \left( 1, \theta, \frac{\pi}{6} \right)\), we can substitute these into their respective formulas.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 1. Compute \(x\):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m x = \rho \sin \phi \cos \theta = 1 \cdot \sin \left( \frac{\pi}{6} \right) \cos \theta = \sin \left( \frac{\pi}{6} \right) \cos \theta = \frac{1}{2} \cos \theta
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 2. Compute \(y\):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m y = \rho \sin \phi \sin \theta = 1 \cdot \sin \left( \frac{\pi}{6} \right) \sin \theta = \sin \left( \frac{\pi}{6} \right) \sin \theta = \frac{1}{2} \sin \theta
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 3. Compute \(z\):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m z = \rho \cos \phi = 1 \cos \left( \frac{\pi}{6} \right) = \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Now, we can see that the Cartesian coordinates are:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m x = \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} \cos \theta, \quad y = \frac{1}{2} \sin \theta, \quad z = \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} \cos \phi
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Since \(\phi = \frac{\pi}{6}\), we can substitute \(\cos \phi = \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\) and \(\sin \phi = \frac{1}{2}\) into the expression for \(x\):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m x = \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} \cos \theta
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m The radius of this circle is the distance from the center of the circle (which is at \(\theta = 0\)) to any point on the circle. This distance is given by \(2x\):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 2x = 2 \cdot \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} \cos \theta = \sqrt{3} \cos \theta
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m We need to find \(\cos \theta\) when \(\phi = \frac{\pi}{6}\):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \cos \left( \frac{\pi}{6} \right) = \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Therefore,
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 2x = \sqrt{3} \cdot \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} = \frac{3}{2}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m The radius of the circle is:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \boxed{\frac{3}{2}}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m [Ground Truth]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \frac{1}{2}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m [Is Boxed Matched]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m True
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
