[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To solve this problem, we need to determine the greatest common factor (GCF) of two numbers, 40 and 48.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Step 1: Find the prime factorization of each number.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m - The prime factorization of 40: \(40 = 2 \times 2 \times 2 \times 5\) (or simplified as \(2^3 \times 5\))
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m - The prime factorization of 48: \(48 = 2 \times 2 \times 2 \times 2 \times 3\) (or simplified as \(2^4 \times 3\))
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Step 2: Identify the common prime factors and their lowest powers.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m In this case, the only common prime factor is 2.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m - The lowest power of 2 in both factorizations is \(2^3\).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Step 3: Multiply the common prime factor raised to the lowest power to get the GCF.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m - The GCF is \(2^3 = 8\).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m So, the greatest common factor of 40 and 48 is \(\boxed{8}\).<|endoftext|>
