[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m From these details, we can determine:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m - \(a = 1\) (since the parabola opens upward and the vertex is \((1, 0.5)\))
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m - \(c = 0\) (since the point \((2, 0)\) lies on the parabola, and it implies a y-intercept of \(y = 0\))
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Now we have the values \(a = 1\) and \(c = 0\). The expression \(a - b + c\) becomes \(1 - b + 0 = 1 - b\).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To find \(b\), we use the information from the vertex and the point \((2, 0)\). Since the function passes through \((2, 0)\), the value at \(x = 2\) should match \(y = 0\):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ y(2) = a(2)^2 + b(2) + c = 1(2)^2 + b(2) + 0 = 0 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ 4 + 2b = 0 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ 2b = -4 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ b = -2 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m So, \(a = 1\) and \(b = -2\). Therefore, \(a - b + c = 1 - (-2) + 0 = 1 + 2 + 0 = 3\).
