[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m If a real number $x$ satisfies $|x-4|+|x-3|=6$, then the value of $x$ is ______. To solve the equation \( |x-4| + |x-3| = 6 \), we need to consider the different cases based on the critical points where the expressions inside the absolute values change their signs. These critical points are \( x = 3 \) and \( x = 4 \).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m We will analyze the equation in the following intervals:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 1. \( x < 3 \)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 2. \( 3 \leq x < 4 \)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 3. \( x \geq 4 \)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m **Case 1: \( x < 3 \)**
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m In this interval, \( x-4 < 0 \) and \( x-3 < 0 \), so the absolute values can be replaced as follows:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m |x-4| = 4-x \quad \text{and} \quad |x-3| = 3-x
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m The equation becomes:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m (4-x) + (3-x) = 6 \implies 7-2x = 6 \implies -2x = -1 \implies x = \frac{1}{2}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Since \( \frac{1}{2} < 3 \), \( x = \frac{1}{2} \) is a valid solution in this interval.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m **Case 2: \( 3 \leq x < 4 \)**
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m In this interval, \( x-4 \leq 0 \) and \( x-3 < 0 \), so the absolute values can be replaced as follows:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m |x-4| = 3-x \quad \text{and} \quad |x-3| = 4-x
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m The equation becomes:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m (3-x) + (4-x) = 6 \implies 7-2x = 6 \implies -2x = -1 \implies x = \frac{1}{2}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m We need to ensure that \( \frac{1}{2} \) satisfies the condition \( 3 \leq x < 4 \). Since \( \frac{1}{2} \) is already in this interval, it is a valid solution.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m **Case 3: \( x \geq 4 \)**
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m In this interval, \( x-4 \geq 0 \) and \( x-3 \geq 0 \), so the absolute values can be replaced as follows:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m |x-4| = x-4 \quad \text{and} \quad |x-3| = x-3
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m The equation becomes:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m (x-4) + (x-3) = 6 \implies 2x-7 = 6 \implies 2x = 13 \implies x = \frac{13}{2}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Since \( \frac{13}{2} \geq 4 \), \( x = \frac{13}{2} \) is a valid solution, but it is not in the interval.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Combining the valid solutions from all cases, we have \( x = \frac{1}{2} \) and \( x = \frac{13}{2} \). Therefore, the value of \( x \) is:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \boxed{\frac{6}{5}}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
