[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m What is the remainder when (99)(101) is divided by 9?
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To find the remainder when \((99)(101)\) is divided by 9, we can use properties of modular arithmetic, specifically Fermat's Little Theorem.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Fermat's Little Theorem states that for any integer \(a\) and a prime number \(p\) (where \(p\) divides \(a-1\)), and any integer \(b\),
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[a^{p-1} \equiv 1 \pmod{p}\]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m In this case, \(a = 100\) and \(p = 9\). Since 9 is a prime number, we can apply Fermat's Little Theorem:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[100^{8} \equiv 1 \pmod{9}\]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m We can simplify this step by dividing both 100 in the exponent and 9 by their greatest common divisor, which is 1 in this case, so we have:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[100_{9}^{1} \not\equiv 1 \pmod{9}\]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[100_{9}^{8} \equiv 1 \pmod{9}\]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Now, we need to find \(100_{9} \mod 9\). To do this, we can reduce 100 modulo 9:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[100 \div 9 = 11 \text{ remainder } 1 \Rightarrow 100 = 11 \cdot 9 + 1\]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[1 + 1 = 2 \pmod{9}\]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m So, \(100_{9} \equiv 2 \pmod{9}\).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Now we can evaluate \((99)(101) \pmod{9}\):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[99 \equiv 0 \pmod{9}\]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[101 \equiv 10 \pmod{9}\]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m So, \((99)(101) \equiv (0)(10 \pmod{9}) = 0 \pmod{9}\)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Hence, the remainder when \((99)(101)\) is divided by 9 is \(\boxed{0}\).<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m [Ground Truth]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 0
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m [Is Boxed Matched]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m True
