[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m You have three shirts and four pairs of pants.  How many outfits consisting of one shirt and one pair of pants can you make?
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To determine the total number of possible outfits consisting of one shirt and one pair of pants from the given options (5 shirts and 4 pairs of pants), we can use the fundamental principle of counting. This principle states that if there are \( m \) ways to do one thing and \( n \) ways to do another, then the total number of ways to do both is \( m \times n \).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Here, we have:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m - 5 shirts
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m - 4 pairs of pants
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m So, the total number of possible outfits is calculated as follows:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 5 \text{ shirts} \times 4 \text{ pairs of pants} = 20 \text{ outfits}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Therefore, the number of possible outfits is \(\boxed{20}\).<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m [Ground Truth]
