[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To solve this problem, we need to find the greatest two-digit whole number whose digits multiply to 8. Let's list the possible pairs of digits (a, b) such that \(a \times b = 8\):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 1. \(1 \times 8 = 8\)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 2. \(2 \times 4 = 8\)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 3. \(3 \times 2 = 6\) (not a two-digit number, so this pair is not valid)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 4. \(4 \times 2 = 8\) (only valid two-digit number)
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m So, the valid pairs are (1, 8) and (2, 4).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Among these, the greatest two-digit whole number is 84. Let's write a Python code to verify this.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m ```python
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m # Finding the greatest two-digit whole number whose digits multiply to 8
