[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m A spring with a rest length of 25.3 cm is pulled down an additional 2.45 cm, then released. Determine the frequency and period of motion for this spring.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m If the spring is released from its initial position and vibrates with an amplitude of 0.450 m maximum, find its frequency and period.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To determine the frequency and period of the spring's oscillations, we start with the frequency formula:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ f = \frac{1}{2\pi} \sqrt{\frac{\mu}{k}} \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m where \( g = \frac{4\pi^2}{5} \) is the.gravity constant.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m First, we calculate the frequency:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 1. **Calculate the speed of the mass:**
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ v = g s e t = (9.8 \frac{m}{s^2} \times 2.45 \frac{m}{s}) = 23.41 \frac{m}{s} \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 2. **Calculate the frequency, \( f \), using the speed and gravitational acceleration:**
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ f = \frac{1}{2\pi} \sqrt{\frac{5}{23.41}} = \frac{1}{2\pi} \times \frac{\sqrt{5}}{\sqrt{23.41}} = \frac{\sqrt{10}}{2 \times 3.142 \times 1.526} = \frac{\sqrt{10}}{9.694} = 0.332 \frac{rad}{s} \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Next, we determine the period of the oscillation, \( T \):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ T = 2\pi \sqrt{\frac{L}{g}} \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m where \( L \) is the length of the spring. We are given \( L = 25.3 \text{ cm} = 0.253 \text{ m} \):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ T = 2\pi \sqrt{\frac{0.253}{9.8 \frac{m}{s^2}}} = 2\pi \sqrt{0.02574} = 2\pi \times 0.05077 = 3.142 \times 0.05077 = 0.1666 \text{ s} = 8.04 \text{ ms} \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Now, we find the frequency and period for each part of the problem.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m **Frequency:**
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m - **For the spring stretched an additional 2.45 cm (0.0245 m):**
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ f_{\text{stretch}} = \frac{1}{2\pi} \sqrt{\frac{5}{23.41}} \times \frac{25.3}{0.0245} = 0.332 \times \frac{25.3}{0.0245 \times 0.986} = 0.332 \times \frac{25.3}{0.022} = 33.2 \times 117.05 = 3941.1 \text{ Hz} \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m **Frequency:**
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m - **For the minimum distance traveled (0.450 m):**
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ f_{\text{minimum}} = \frac{1}{2\pi} \sqrt{\frac{5}{23.41}} \times \frac{0.253}{0.450} = 0.332 \times \frac{0.253}{0.450 \times 0.986} = 0.332 \times \frac{0.253}{0.441}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m [Ground Truth]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 1
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m [Is Boxed Matched]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m True
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
