[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To determine the cost of five identical CDs, we start by using the information given: two CDs cost a total of $28. We can express the cost of one CD in terms of the quantity \( c \) (in equivalent units), which means:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ \text{Total cost of two CDs} = 28 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m From this, we can determine the cost of one CD:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ c \times \text{Cost per CD} = 28 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m The cost per CD then becomes:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ c \times c = c^2 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m We know that one CD costs $0.64, so we can set up the equation:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ c^2 = 0.64 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To find the cost of five CDs, we first solve for \( c \):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ c = \sqrt{0.64} \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ c = 0.8 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m So, the cost of one CD is $0.80. To find the cost of five CDs, we multiply the cost of one CD by 5:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ \text{Cost of five CDs} = 5 \times 0.8 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ \text{Cost of five CDs} = 4 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Therefore, the cost of five CDs is:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ \boxed{4} \]<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
