[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To determine the number of possible distinct arrangements of the letters in the word "DOG", we need to calculate the number of permutations of the string.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m The formula for permutations (also known as arrangements) is given by:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ P(n, r) = \frac{n!}{(n-r)!} \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m where \( n \) is the total number of items to arrange, and \( r \) is the number of items to arrange at a time.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m In this case, we have 4 letters in the word "DOG", so \( n = 4 \). We need to arrange these 4 letters such that the letters appear in the same order as in the word "DOG" (i.e., "DOG" itself):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ P(4, 4) = \frac{4!}{(4-4)!} = \frac{4!}{0!} \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m First, calculate the factorial of 4 (which is \( 4! = 4 \times 3 \times 2 \times 1 = 24 \)):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ 4! = 4 \times 3 \times 2 \times 1 = 24 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Since \((0!)\) is defined as \(1\), the expression simplifies to:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ \frac{4!}{0!} = \frac{24}{1} = 24 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m So, the number of possible distinct arrangements of the letters in the word "DOG" is:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \boxed{24}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
