[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m A standard die is rolled six times. What is the probability that the product of all six rolls is odd? Express your answer as a common fraction.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To determine the probability that the product of all six rolls of a standard die is odd, we need to analyze the conditions under which the product is odd. A product is odd if and only if all the factors of the product are odd. Therefore, we need to ensure that each number rolled on the die is odd.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m A standard die has six faces, each with a number from 1 to 6. The numbers are only divisible by 1 and 2, so they are not odd. To get an odd product, each die roll must be odd.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m The odd numbers among the numbers on a standard die are 1, 3, and 5. Therefore, there are 3 faces that must be odd out of the 6 faces.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m The probability that any single roll results in an odd number is:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \frac{\text{Number of faces that are odd}}{\text{Total number of faces}} = \frac{3}{6} = \frac{1}{2}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Since the outcome of each roll is independent of the others, the probability that all six rolls are odd is the product of the probabilities of each individual roll being odd:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \left(\frac{1}{2}\right) \times \left(\frac{1}{2}\right) \times \left(\frac{1}{2}\right) \times \left(\frac{1}{2}\right) \times \left(\frac{1}{2}\right) \times \left(\frac{1}{2}\right) = \left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^6 = \frac{1}{64}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Thus, the probability that the product of all six rolls is odd is:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \boxed{\frac{1}{64}}
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m [Ground Truth]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \frac{1}{64}
