[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To determine the domain of the function \(a(x) = \sqrt{3^x - 7^x}\), we need to ensure that the expression inside the square root is non-negative. This is because the square root of a negative number is not defined in the real number system, and it is also not considered in the set of entire functions, which are those that can be expressed using rational functions.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m So, we need to solve the inequality:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[3^x - 7^x \geq 0.\]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Let's rewrite \(7^x\) as \(3^x \cdot 7^{-x}\):
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[3^x - 3^x \cdot 7^{-x} \geq 0.\]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[3^x (1 - 7^{-x}) \geq 0.\]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Since \(1 - 7^{-x}\) is always positive (because \(-7^{-x}\) is always less than 1), we can divide both sides of the inequality by \(1 - 7^{-x}\) without changing the direction of the inequality:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[3^x \geq 0.\]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m The exponential function \(3^x\) is always positive for any real number \(x\). Therefore, the inequality \(3^x \geq 0\) is satisfied for all real numbers \(x\).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Thus, the domain of the function \(a(x) = \sqrt{3^x - 7^x}\) is all real numbers, which can be written in interval notation as \(\boxed{(-\infty, \infty)}\).<|endoftext|>
