[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m To solve this problem, we first need to understand how factors relate to the prime factorization of a number.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m A positive integer can have at most as many distinct positive integer factors as the largest possible exponent in its prime factorization. This is because factors of a number \( n \) include 1 and itself, so their count already includes all prime factors (counted as many times as they appear) plus the number of each prime factor raised to some power, plus one for the case of unity (which can be any exponent).
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Let's find the prime factorization of each number less than 20:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
