[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Find $n$ if $n\cdot n! + n! = 720$, where $n! = n\cdot (n-1)\cdot (n-2)\cdots 2\cdot 1$.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Let's solve for $n$ in the equation $n \cdot n! + n! = 720$.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m First, we rewrite $n!$ in factorial notation:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ n! = n \cdot (n-1) \cdot (n-2) \cdots 2 \cdot 1 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m So the equation becomes:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ n \cdot (n \cdot (n-1) \cdot (n-2) \cdots 2 \cdot 1) + n \cdot (n-1) \cdot (n-2) \cdots 2 \cdot 1 = 720 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m We can factor out $n \cdot (n-1) \cdot (n-2) \cdots 2 \cdot 1$ from the left side:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ n \cdot (n-1) \cdot (n-2) \cdots 2 \cdot 1 \left( 1 + n \right) = 720 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Let $n! = k$. Then the equation becomes:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ k \cdot k! + k! = 720 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ k \cdot (k+1) \cdot (k+2) \cdots 3 \cdot 2 \cdot 1 = 720 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m We know that $k! > k \cdot k \cdot (k+1) \cdot (k+2) \cdot \ldots \cdot n$, so we can infer that $k \leq 7$. We can test values of $k$ from $1$ to $7$ to find the correct $n$.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Let's test $k = 5$:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ 5 \cdot 5! + 5! = 5 \cdot 120 + 120 = 600 + 120 = 720 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ 5! = 5 \cdot 4 \cdot 3 \cdot 2 \cdot 1 = 120 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ 5 \cdot (5+1) \cdot (5+2) \cdots (5+7) = 5 \cdot 6 \cdot 7 \cdot 8 \cdot 9 \cdot 10 \cdot 11 \cdot 12 \cdot 13 \cdot 14 \cdot 15 \cdot 16 \cdot 17 \cdot 18 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Since $17 \cdot 18 > 120$, we continue the factorization to 19, 20, 21, 22, and so on.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m Testing $5 \cdot 10 \cdot 11 \cdot 12 \cdot 13 \cdot 14 \cdot 15 \cdot 16 \cdot 17 \cdot 18 \cdot 19 \cdot 20 \cdot 21 \cdot 22$ gives:
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m \[ 5 \cdot 10 \cdot 11 \cdot 12 \cdot 13 \cdot 14 \cdot 15 \cdot 16 \cdot 17 \cdot 18 \cdot 19 \cdot 20 \cdot 21 \cdot 22 \equiv 0 \cdot 3 \cdot 2 \cdot 1 \]
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m This shows that $5 \cdot 5! - 20 \cdot 5! + 5 \cdot 5! - 21 \cdot 5! + 5 \cdot 5! - 22 \cdot 5! + 5 \cdot 5! - 23 \cdot 5! = 720$.
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m So the value of $n$ is:
