[36m(main_task pid=2326006)[0m This problem can be solved by using the concept of circular permutations. In the specific case of a round table, we need to consider the rotational symmetry of the table. The number of ways to arrange \( n \) people around a table is given by \((n-1)!\), which represents the factorial of \( n-1 \), since the table is symmetric about a central point.
